{"D288/ch1/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the steps, in order</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 178" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fa" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text-3)"/></marker></defs><rect x="12" y="10" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.12" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="20" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">1</text><text x="71" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">new Account</text><line x1="130" y1="38" x2="164" y2="38" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="166" y="10" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="174" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">2</text><text x="225" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Fields are</text><text x="225" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">initialized to…</text><line x1="284" y1="38" x2="318" y2="38" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="320" y="10" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="328" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">3</text><text x="379" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Constructor body</text><text x="379" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">runs</text><line x1="438" y1="38" x2="472" y2="38" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="474" y="10" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="482" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">4</text><text x="533" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Reference</text><text x="533" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">returned to the…</text><path d="M533 66 L533 80 L71 80 L71 102" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="12" y="94" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="20" y="108" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">5</text><text x="71" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Object lives as</text><text x="71" y="138" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">long as reachable…</text><line x1="130" y1="122" x2="164" y2="122" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="166" y="94" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="174" y="108" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">6</text><text x="225" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Once unreachable,</text><text x="225" y="138" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">the garbage…</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-oop"><h3>📖 Worked example — a well-encapsulated class, used</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — Customer with guarded state</div><pre>public class Customer {
    private String name;                 // hidden
    private int loyaltyPoints;

    public Customer(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public void addPoints(int pts) {
        if (pts &gt; 0) loyaltyPoints += pts;   // the class enforces its rules
    }
    public int getLoyaltyPoints() { return loyaltyPoints; }
}

Customer c = new Customer("Amara");
c.addPoints(50);
c.addPoints(-10);                        // silently refused
System.out.println(c.getLoyaltyPoints()); // 50</pre>Private fields + public methods = the object can never hold an invalid state, because every change passes through validating code. That&rsquo;s the encapsulation answer back-end questions look for.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ WHY IT MATTERS IN A BACK END</div>Entities and DTOs cross layers constantly; if fields were public, any layer could corrupt them. Encapsulation is what makes the service layer the ONLY place business rules live.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
<div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
<p>Java's object-oriented core is the foundation everything else in D288 rests on. Spring Boot wires together objects; JPA maps them to tables; controllers receive and produce them. Without solid command of classes, references, and encapsulation, every later layer compounds confusion. This section is the heart of OO Java — what a class IS, what an object is, why fields are private, and how getters/setters and constructors give controlled access.</p>
</div>

<h3>Class as blueprint, object as instance</h3>

<p>A class declares fields and methods. An object is an actual instance with concrete state. Many objects can exist of the same class — each with its own field values, all sharing the same methods.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class Dog {
    String name;
    int ageYears;

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " says woof!");
    }
}

Dog d1 = new Dog();
d1.name = "Rex";
d1.ageYears = 4;

Dog d2 = new Dog();
d2.name = "Mochi";
d2.ageYears = 2;

d1.bark();  // "Rex says woof!"
d2.bark();  // "Mochi says woof!"
</code></pre>

<p>Two objects, two sets of state, one shared method definition. The <code>new</code> keyword allocates memory on the heap and returns a reference.</p>

<h3>References, not values</h3>

<p>Java variables that hold objects actually hold REFERENCES (pointers, conceptually). Multiple references can point to the same object.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>Dog a = new Dog();
a.name = "Rex";
Dog b = a;          // b points to the SAME dog
b.name = "Buddy";
System.out.println(a.name);  // "Buddy" — a and b are aliases
</code></pre>

<p>To make a real copy, you'd need to construct a new Dog and copy fields over (or use a copy constructor).</p>

<h3>Encapsulation — keep fields private</h3>

<p>Bad — fields are public:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class Account {
    public double balance;  // anyone can set this to anything
}

Account a = new Account();
a.balance = -1000;  // negative balance! bug waiting to happen
</code></pre>

<p>Good — fields private, access through methods:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class Account {
    private double balance;

    public double getBalance() { return balance; }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount &lt;= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Must be positive");
        balance += amount;
    }

    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount &gt; balance) throw new IllegalStateException("Insufficient funds");
        balance -= amount;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p><strong>Encapsulation</strong> = hiding internal state behind a controlled interface. The class enforces invariants (balance can't go negative through normal operations) because it controls who modifies the field.</p>

<h3>Constructors — initialize state at creation</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class Account {
    private double balance;
    private String owner;

    public Account(String owner, double initialDeposit) {
        if (initialDeposit &lt; 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("No negative deposits");
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balance = initialDeposit;
    }
}

Account a = new Account("Natasha", 1000);
</code></pre>

<p>A constructor has no return type and the SAME NAME as the class. It runs once when an object is created and ensures the object starts in a valid state. If you don't define any, Java gives you a no-arg default. If you DO define one (taking parameters), the default disappears — define a no-arg yourself if you also want it.</p>

<h3>this keyword</h3>

<p>Inside an instance method or constructor, <code>this</code> refers to the current object. Used to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Disambiguate fields from parameters with the same name (<code>this.balance = balance</code>)</li>
<li>Pass the current object to another method (<code>doSomething(this)</code>)</li>
<li>Call another constructor in the same class (<code>this(...)</code>)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Constructor chaining</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class Account {
    private String owner;
    private double balance;

    public Account(String owner, double balance) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public Account(String owner) {
        this(owner, 0.0);   // delegate to the other constructor
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Reuse: each variant funnels through ONE master constructor that contains all validation.</p>

<h3>Getter/setter convention</h3>

<p>Java's JavaBeans convention: for a private field <code>balance</code>, expose:</p>

<ul>
<li><code>public double getBalance()</code> — the getter (reads the value)</li>
<li><code>public void setBalance(double balance)</code> — the setter (writes the value)</li>
</ul>

<p>For boolean fields, getter is <code>isXxx()</code> instead of <code>getXxx()</code>: <code>isActive()</code> not <code>getActive()</code>. JPA, Jackson, and Spring all assume this convention.</p>

<p>Don't generate a setter for every field automatically — only for fields that genuinely should be mutable. Many fields (like <code>createdAt</code> or <code>id</code>) should be read-only after construction.</p>

<h3>toString, equals, hashCode</h3>

<p>Every Java class extends <code>Object</code> and inherits three commonly-overridden methods:</p>

<ul>
<li><code>toString()</code> — string representation, used in logging and debugging</li>
<li><code>equals(Object o)</code> — value equality test (default is reference equality, almost never what you want)</li>
<li><code>hashCode()</code> — hash for use in HashMap and HashSet (must be consistent with equals)</li>
</ul>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@Override
public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof Account)) return false;
    Account other = (Account) o;
    return Objects.equals(this.owner, other.owner) &amp;&amp; this.balance == other.balance;
}

@Override
public int hashCode() {
    return Objects.hash(owner, balance);
}

@Override
public String toString() {
    return "Account{owner='" + owner + "', balance=" + balance + "}";
}
</code></pre>

<p>Rule: if you override <code>equals</code>, you MUST override <code>hashCode</code> consistently (equal objects must have equal hash codes). IDEs generate these for you.</p>

<h3>Records (Java 16+)</h3>

<p>For simple data carriers, records auto-generate constructor, getters, equals, hashCode, and toString:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public record Point(int x, int y) {}

Point p = new Point(3, 4);
p.x();  // accessor (no get prefix)
p.y();
</code></pre>

<p>Records are IMMUTABLE — fields are final, no setters. Use them anywhere you'd previously hand-roll a value class.</p>

<h3>Object lifecycle</h3>

<ol>
<li><code>new Account(...)</code> allocates memory on the heap</li>
<li>Fields are initialized to defaults (0 / null / false)</li>
<li>Constructor body runs</li>
<li>Reference returned to the caller</li>
<li>Object lives as long as reachable from any GC root</li>
<li>Once unreachable, the garbage collector reclaims it (eventually)</li>
</ol>

<p>You never <code>delete</code> objects in Java. The GC handles memory automatically. This eliminates entire classes of bugs (use-after-free, double-free, memory leaks from forgotten frees) at the cost of some runtime overhead.</p>

<div class="callout callout-recall">
<div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div>
<ul>
<li>A class is a blueprint; objects are instances created with <code>new</code></li>
<li>Object variables hold REFERENCES, not values; assignment aliases, doesn't copy</li>
<li>Encapsulation: make fields private, expose them via methods that enforce invariants</li>
<li>Constructors initialize state; same name as class, no return type</li>
<li><code>this</code> is the current object inside instance methods</li>
<li>Constructor chaining (<code>this(...)</code>) reduces duplication</li>
<li>JavaBeans convention: <code>getX/setX</code> for fields, <code>isX</code> for booleans</li>
<li>Override <code>toString</code>, <code>equals</code>, and <code>hashCode</code> for value semantics</li>
<li>Records (Java 16+) auto-generate boilerplate for immutable data classes</li>
<li>The garbage collector reclaims unreachable objects automatically</li>
</ul>
</div>
"},"D288/ch1/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — how this section fits together</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Inheritance,…</text><rect x="282" y="18" width="76" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">extends</text><rect x="484" y="64" width="87" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Overriding</text><rect x="477" y="156" width="101" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Polymorphism</text><rect x="230" y="202" width="179" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">super — call the parent</text><rect x="47" y="156" width="130" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">abstract classes</text><rect x="69" y="64" width="87" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Interfaces</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-poly"><h3>📖 Worked example — interface-driven polymorphism in a back end</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — swap implementations without touching callers</div><pre>public interface PriceCalculator { double price(Order o); }

public class StandardPricing implements PriceCalculator {
    public double price(Order o) { return o.subtotal(); }
}
public class HolidayPricing implements PriceCalculator {
    public double price(Order o) { return o.subtotal() * 0.9; }
}

PriceCalculator calc = new HolidayPricing();   // the ONLY line that changes
System.out.println(calc.price(order));          // 10% off, callers untouched</pre>Callers depend on the INTERFACE; the concrete class can change per season, per test, per config. This is exactly the shape Spring&rsquo;s dependency injection automates — declare the interface, let the container hand you an implementation.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ PROGRAM TO THE INTERFACE</div>Declaring <code>PriceCalculator calc</code> (not HolidayPricing) is the graded habit: it enables swapping, mocking in tests, and DI. &ldquo;Program to an interface, not an implementation.&rdquo;</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
<div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
<p>Service classes implement business interfaces; Spring injects implementations through interface types. Without command of inheritance and polymorphism, you're using the framework without understanding it. Inheritance lets one class share another's behavior; polymorphism lets your code work uniformly on different concrete types behind a common abstraction. Interfaces define contracts. This trio is the heart of object-oriented design — and the heart of how Spring, JPA, and most large Java codebases organize themselves.</p>
</div>

<h3>extends — inheritance from a class</h3>

<p>One class can inherit fields and methods from another using <code>extends</code>:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class Animal {
    protected String name;
    public void sleep() { System.out.println(name + " is sleeping"); }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    public void bark() { System.out.println(name + " says woof!"); }
}

Dog d = new Dog();
d.name = "Rex";   // inherited field
d.sleep();         // inherited method
d.bark();          // own method
</code></pre>

<p>Java has SINGLE inheritance — a class can extend exactly one parent. This avoids the "diamond problem" of multiple inheritance.</p>

<h3>Overriding — child changes parent behavior</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class Animal {
    public void speak() { System.out.println("Some sound"); }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void speak() { System.out.println("Woof!"); }
}

public class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void speak() { System.out.println("Meow!"); }
}
</code></pre>

<p>The <code>@Override</code> annotation tells the compiler "I'm intentionally overriding the parent's method." If you misspell the method name, the compiler catches it instead of silently introducing a new method.</p>

<h3>Polymorphism — many forms, one interface</h3>

<p>The MAGIC happens when you store children in a parent-typed variable:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>Animal[] zoo = { new Dog(), new Cat(), new Dog() };
for (Animal a : zoo) {
    a.speak();   // calls Dog.speak() or Cat.speak() based on actual type
}
// Output:
// Woof!
// Meow!
// Woof!
</code></pre>

<p>The compiler sees the static type <code>Animal</code> and verifies that <code>.speak()</code> is callable. At runtime, the JVM dispatches to the actual object's method (Dog's or Cat's). This is <strong>dynamic dispatch</strong> — the heart of polymorphism.</p>

<h3>super — call the parent</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void speak() {
        super.speak();  // call Animal.speak() first
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>In a constructor, <code>super(...)</code> calls the parent's constructor. If you don't call it explicitly, Java inserts an implicit <code>super()</code> at the start.</p>

<h3>abstract classes — partial implementation</h3>

<p>An abstract class CANNOT be instantiated directly. It typically has abstract methods that subclasses must implement.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public abstract class Shape {
    public abstract double area();   // must be implemented by subclass

    public void describe() {
        System.out.println("This shape has area " + area());
    }
}

public class Circle extends Shape {
    private double radius;
    public Circle(double r) { this.radius = r; }
    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}

Shape s = new Shape();    // COMPILE ERROR
Shape s = new Circle(5);  // OK
s.describe();              // "This shape has area 78.54..."
</code></pre>

<p>Abstract classes are good when subclasses share state AND some behavior, but each needs to specialize a few methods.</p>

<h3>Interfaces — contracts without implementation</h3>

<p>An interface declares a contract: "these methods will exist." It contains no state.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public interface Comparable&lt;T&gt; {
    int compareTo(T other);
}

public class Money implements Comparable&lt;Money&gt; {
    private long cents;

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Money other) {
        return Long.compare(this.cents, other.cents);
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>A class can <code>implement</code> MANY interfaces (multiple interface inheritance is allowed). This compensates for the single-class inheritance limit.</p>

<h3>Interface vs abstract class</h3>

<table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;">
<thead><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--surface-3);">
<th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Feature</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Interface</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Abstract class</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Inheritance limit</td><td style="padding:8px;">Many (multiple)</td><td style="padding:8px;">One (single)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Can have instance fields</td><td style="padding:8px;">No (only static finals)</td><td style="padding:8px;">Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Can have constructors</td><td style="padding:8px;">No</td><td style="padding:8px;">Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Can have method implementations</td><td style="padding:8px;">Yes (default methods)</td><td style="padding:8px;">Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Designed for</td><td style="padding:8px;">Capability/contract</td><td style="padding:8px;">Shared state + behavior</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Default and static methods on interfaces</h3>

<p>Since Java 8, interfaces can have method implementations:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public interface Greetable {
    String getName();

    // default method — concrete behavior in the interface itself
    default void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello, " + getName());
    }

    // static utility
    static Greetable anonymous() {
        return () -&gt; "Anonymous";  // lambda implementing the single abstract method
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Default methods let you ADD methods to interfaces without breaking existing implementations.</p>

<h3>Programming to interfaces, not implementations</h3>

<p>The classic OO principle: depend on interfaces, not concrete classes. This is what makes code testable and replaceable.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>// Worse — tightly coupled to ArrayList
ArrayList&lt;User&gt; users = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

// Better — uses the interface
List&lt;User&gt; users = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
// Can later swap for LinkedList, CopyOnWriteArrayList, etc.

// Best for services
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;  // interface, not implementation
    public UserService(UserRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
}
</code></pre>

<p>This is exactly how Spring's dependency injection works: services declare interface-typed dependencies, and the IoC container injects the right implementation.</p>

<h3>final classes and methods</h3>

<ul>
<li><code>final class Foo</code> — cannot be extended</li>
<li><code>final void foo()</code> — cannot be overridden</li>
<li><code>final int x = 5</code> — cannot be reassigned</li>
</ul>

<p>Mark classes <code>final</code> when subclassing isn't intended (e.g., <code>String</code> is final). Mark methods <code>final</code> when their behavior must not change.</p>

<h3>instanceof and casting</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>Animal a = getAnimal();
if (a instanceof Dog) {
    Dog d = (Dog) a;
    d.bark();
}

// Java 16+ pattern matching for instanceof:
if (a instanceof Dog d) {
    d.bark();   // 'd' is automatically the casted variable
}
</code></pre>

<p>Heavy use of <code>instanceof</code> often indicates a missed opportunity for polymorphism. If you find yourself branching on the type, see if adding a method to the parent / interface lets each subclass handle its case naturally.</p>

<h3>The Liskov Substitution Principle</h3>

<p>If S is a subclass of T, then objects of T can be replaced with objects of S without breaking the program. Practical implication: subclasses should not violate the parent's contract. A method that overrides shouldn't restrict acceptable inputs or change observable post-conditions in surprising ways.</p>

<div class="callout callout-recall">
<div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div>
<ul>
<li>Single inheritance with <code>extends</code>; multiple interface implementation with <code>implements</code></li>
<li><code>@Override</code> annotation prevents typo bugs when overriding methods</li>
<li>Polymorphism: parent-typed variable, child method called via dynamic dispatch</li>
<li><code>super</code> calls the parent's version (in constructors and overridden methods)</li>
<li>Abstract classes: can't be instantiated; may have abstract methods that subclasses must implement</li>
<li>Interfaces: pure contracts (originally); since Java 8 can also have default and static methods</li>
<li>Interface vs abstract class: interface for capability; abstract class for shared state + behavior</li>
<li>"Program to interfaces, not implementations" — the basis of Spring's dependency injection</li>
<li><code>final</code> prevents extension, overriding, or reassignment</li>
<li>Java 16+ pattern matching for instanceof: <code>if (a instanceof Dog d) d.bark()</code></li>
</ul>
</div>
