.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="166" y="94" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="174" y="108" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">6</text><text x="225" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Optional</text><line x1="284" y1="122" x2="318" y2="122" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="320" y="94" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="328" y="108" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">7</text><text x="379" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Bean is ready to</text><text x="379" y="138" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">use</text><line x1="438" y1="122" x2="472" y2="122" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="474" y="94" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="482" y="108" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">8</text><text x="533" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">On shutdown</text></svg></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
<div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
<p>Inversion of Control (IoC) and Dependency Injection (DI) are the heart of Spring. They're what lets you snap services, repositories, and configuration together without writing wiring code. Master them and Spring becomes obvious. Misunderstand them and Spring feels like magic that occasionally breaks. Every controller, service, and repository you build for D288 will be a Spring bean assembled by the IoC container.</p>
</div>

<h3>What is "Inversion of Control"?</h3>

<p>Normally, your code creates its dependencies:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class UserService {
    private UserRepository repo = new MySQLUserRepository();
    private EmailSender mail = new SMTPEmailSender();
}
</code></pre>

<p>Problems: the class is hard-wired to specific implementations, hard to test (can't substitute fakes), hard to reconfigure.</p>

<p>With IoC, the class DOESN'T create its dependencies. Something else creates them and HANDS THEM IN:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;
    private final EmailSender mail;

    public UserService(UserRepository repo, EmailSender mail) {
        this.repo = repo;
        this.mail = mail;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Control is INVERTED: instead of UserService deciding what to create, the caller (or framework) decides what to pass in. The class is now testable (mock the deps) and reconfigurable (swap implementations) without changing UserService at all.</p>

<h3>The Spring IoC container</h3>

<p>Spring's IoC container is the object that holds, configures, and assembles all your beans. You tell it: "Here are my classes. Here's how they fit together. Build me an application." It instantiates everything in the right order and wires it up.</p>

<p>The container is the <code>ApplicationContext</code>. Spring Boot creates one at startup; you rarely touch it directly.</p>

<h3>What is a bean?</h3>

<p>A bean is any object managed by the Spring container. The container handles:</p>
<ul>
<li>Creating it (calling its constructor)</li>
<li>Wiring its dependencies (injecting other beans)</li>
<li>Managing its lifecycle (initialization, destruction)</li>
<li>Caching it (most beans are singletons — one instance reused everywhere)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Stereotype annotations — marking classes as beans</h3>

<table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;">
<thead><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--surface-3);">
<th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Annotation</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Use for</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>@Component</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Generic Spring-managed bean</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>@Service</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Business-logic class (semantic intent)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>@Repository</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Data-access class; adds JPA exception translation</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>@Controller</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">MVC controller returning view names</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>@RestController</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">REST controller (Controller + ResponseBody)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>@Configuration</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Source of <code>@Bean</code>-method definitions</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>All of these are FUNCTIONALLY EQUIVALENT (they all just mark the class as a bean) but communicate INTENT. Pick the one that matches the class's role.</p>

<h3>Component scanning</h3>

<p>Spring Boot's <code>@SpringBootApplication</code> includes <code>@ComponentScan</code>, which scans the package containing the main class (and all sub-packages) for stereotype-annotated classes. Each one is registered as a bean.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>// src/main/java/com/example/MyApp.java
@SpringBootApplication
public class MyApp { ... }

// src/main/java/com/example/service/UserService.java
@Service
public class UserService { ... }     // auto-discovered

// src/main/java/com/example/repo/UserRepository.java
@Repository
public class UserRepository { ... }   // auto-discovered
</code></pre>

<p>Convention: keep all your application code in or under the same package as your <code>@SpringBootApplication</code> class.</p>

<h3>Constructor injection — the preferred form</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;

    public UserService(UserRepository repo) {  // Spring injects here
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Why constructor injection?</p>
<ul>
<li>Dependencies are <code>final</code> — guaranteed not to change after construction</li>
<li>Cannot create a UserService without providing the deps — no NPE at runtime</li>
<li>Easy to test — just pass mocks in the constructor</li>
<li>If a class has too many constructor args, that's a SIGNAL the class is doing too much</li>
</ul>

<p>Spring 4.3+ doesn't require <code>@Autowired</code> on a constructor when there's only one. Older code may show it explicitly.</p>

<h3>Field injection — discouraged</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@Service
public class UserService {
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository repo;     // works but discouraged
}
</code></pre>

<p>Field injection is concise but bad: dependencies aren't final, can't test without Spring's reflection magic, hides over-coupling. Use constructor injection unless you have a specific reason.</p>

<h3>Setter injection</h3>

<p>For optional dependencies, you can use setter injection:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@Service
public class UserService {
    private NotificationService notifications;

    @Autowired(required = false)
    public void setNotifications(NotificationService n) {
        this.notifications = n;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h3>@Bean methods inside @Configuration</h3>

<p>For beans you DON'T own (third-party classes) or beans that need custom construction:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean
    public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
        ObjectMapper m = new ObjectMapper();
        m.registerModule(new JavaTimeModule());
        return m;
    }

    @Bean
    public RestTemplate restTemplate(RestTemplateBuilder builder) {
        return builder.setConnectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5)).build();
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>The @Bean method's return becomes a bean of that type, available for injection elsewhere.</p>

<h3>Bean scopes</h3>

<table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;">
<thead><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--surface-3);">
<th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Scope</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Meaning</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>singleton</code> (default)</td><td style="padding:8px;">One instance per container; injected wherever needed</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>prototype</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">New instance every time it's injected/requested</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>request</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">One instance per HTTP request</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>session</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">One instance per HTTP session</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>99% of beans should be singletons. Use other scopes only when you have a clear reason.</p>

<h3>The bean lifecycle</h3>

<ol>
<li>Container starts up</li>
<li>For each bean, container looks at its annotations and config</li>
<li>Container resolves the dependency graph (which beans need which)</li>
<li>Container instantiates beans in correct order (deps first)</li>
<li>Container injects dependencies via constructors / setters / fields</li>
<li>Optional: any <code>@PostConstruct</code> methods are called</li>
<li>Bean is ready to use</li>
<li>On shutdown: <code>@PreDestroy</code> methods are called</li>
</ol>

<h3>Avoiding circular dependencies</h3>

<p>If A depends on B and B depends on A, the container can't decide who to create first → startup failure.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>Caused by: BeanCurrentlyInCreationException:
  Error creating bean with name 'a': Requested bean is currently in creation:
  Is there an unresolvable circular reference?
</code></pre>

<p>Fix: extract a third bean that both depend on, or rethink whether one of A/B should really depend on the other.</p>

<h3>Qualifying ambiguous beans</h3>

<p>If multiple beans implement the same interface, Spring won't know which to inject:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@Service
public class EmailSender implements MessageSender { ... }

@Service
public class SmsSender implements MessageSender { ... }

@Service
public class NotificationService {
    public NotificationService(MessageSender sender) { ... }
    // ERROR: 2 candidates for MessageSender
}
</code></pre>

<p>Resolve with <code>@Qualifier</code> or <code>@Primary</code>:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@Service
@Primary       // this one wins when injection is ambiguous
public class EmailSender implements MessageSender { ... }

// Or qualify at the injection site:
public NotificationService(@Qualifier("smsSender") MessageSender sender) { ... }
</code></pre>

<h3>The IoC mental model</h3>

<p>Think of your application as a graph of beans, where edges are dependencies. Spring's container reads the annotations, builds the graph, and constructs each node in dependency order. Your code never says <code>new</code> for application-level objects (only for value objects like DTOs). Everything else flows through the container.</p>

<div class="callout callout-recall">
<div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Inversion of Control:</strong> classes don't create their dependencies — something else hands them in</li>
<li><strong>Dependency Injection:</strong> the mechanism for "handing in" — constructor, setter, or field</li>
<li>A bean is any object managed by Spring's IoC container (<code>ApplicationContext</code>)</li>
<li>Stereotype annotations (@Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller) mark classes for component scanning</li>
<li>Component scanning auto-registers stereotype-annotated classes in the same package tree as <code>@SpringBootApplication</code></li>
<li><strong>Prefer constructor injection</strong> — explicit, testable, makes deps final</li>
<li><code>@Configuration</code> classes with <code>@Bean</code> methods define beans for third-party classes</li>
<li>Default scope is singleton; prototype/request/session exist for special cases</li>
<li>Circular dependencies cause startup failure — extract a third bean or refactor</li>
<li>Use <code>@Qualifier</code> or <code>@Primary</code> when multiple beans implement the same interface</li>
</ul>
</div>
"},"D288/ch2/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Application Properties &amp;…</text><rect x="252" y="18" width="137" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">no code recompile</text><rect x="438" y="64" width="179" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">application.properties…</text><rect x="433" y="156" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Configuring the datasource</text><rect x="234" y="202" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">JPA-related properties</text><rect x="47" y="156" width="130" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@Value injection</text><rect x="26" y="64" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Environment variables…</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-props"><h3>📖 Worked example — wiring MySQL through application.properties</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — the datasource block, line by line</div><pre>spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/shopdb
spring.datasource.username=root
spring.datasource.password=secret
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
server.port=8080</pre>Line 1 names the driver (mysql), host, port (3306 = MySQL&rsquo;s default), and database. Lines 2-3 authenticate. ddl-auto=update lets Hibernate evolve tables from your entities. Change any of these and restart — <b>no code recompile</b>. Wrong password or DB name shows up as a startup failure mentioning the DataSource: read that stack trace top-down, the cause is in the first lines.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ PORTS: 8080 vs 3306</div>server.port is YOUR app&rsquo;s HTTP port; 3306 inside the JDBC URL is the DATABASE&rsquo;s port. Mixing them up is the classic can&rsquo;t-connect misdiagnosis.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Hard-coded values in Java code mean every environment change is a recompile. Spring Boot's configuration system externalizes these to properties files, environment variables, and profiles. Done right, the same JAR runs in dev, test, and production with no code changes.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. application.properties basics.</strong> Lives in src/main/resources. Key=value format. server.port=8080, spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/appdb. Spring Boot reads at startup and applies values throughout.</p>
<p><strong>2. Configuring the datasource.</strong> spring.datasource.url, spring.datasource.username, spring.datasource.password, spring.datasource.driver-class-name. For MySQL: jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/appdb, com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver. For H2 (dev): jdbc:h2:mem:testdb, org.h2.Driver. The PA's local-vs-lab setup differs only in these properties.</p>
<p><strong>3. JPA-related properties.</strong> spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto controls schema management — none, validate, update, create, create-drop. spring.jpa.show-sql=true logs every SQL Hibernate emits (useful for debugging). spring.jpa.database-platform names the dialect (org.hibernate.dialect.MySQL8Dialect).</p>
<p><strong>4. @Value injection.</strong> Inject single properties into beans: @Value("${app.greeting}") private String greeting. Quick for one-off values; awkward for grouped config. Supports default values: @Value("${app.timeout:30}") falls back to 30.</p>
<p><strong>5. Profiles for environment differences.</strong> application-dev.properties, application-prod.properties. Activate with spring.profiles.active=prod. The active profile's file overrides values in the base application.properties. Standard pattern for dev/test/prod separation.</p>
<p><strong>6. Environment variables override.</strong> Spring Boot reads from many sources in priority order: command-line args > env variables > application-{profile}.properties > application.properties. SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD env var overrides the file. Production deployments typically supply secrets via env vars, not files.</p>
<p><strong>7. Don't commit secrets.</strong> DB passwords, API keys, JWT secrets — never commit. Use environment variables, secrets managers, or .gitignore'd files. application.properties gets you started; production needs more discipline. The PA's local config is fine in development; never replicate that pattern with real production credentials.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Hard-coding configuration values in Java instead of in application.properties.</li>
 <li>Forgetting to activate a profile in production — dev defaults leak through.</li>
 <li>Committing application.properties with real database passwords or keys to git.</li>
 <li>Mixing @Value and @ConfigurationProperties for the same property group — confusing.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>application.properties externalizes configuration. Datasource and JPA properties drive the database connection. @Value injects single values; profiles handle environment variants. Environment variables override file-based config. Keep secrets out of source control. Same JAR runs in dev and prod by changing properties, not code.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">application.properties</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Boot's main configuration file; key=value format.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Datasource</span></dt>
 <dd>Connection settings for a database: URL, username, password, driver.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">ddl-auto</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Boot setting controlling schema management on startup.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Value</span></dt>
 <dd>Injects a single property value into a bean.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Spring profile</span></dt>
 <dd>Named configuration variant (dev, test, prod) activated at startup.</dd>
</dl>"},"D288/ch2/s4":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Spring Boot Project…</text><rect x="241" y="18" width="158" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Maven project layout</text><rect x="488" y="64" width="80" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">gitignore</text><rect x="433" y="156" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">The standard package layout</text><rect x="225" y="202" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Why the layered structure</text><rect x="17" y="156" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">The main application class</text><rect x="54" y="64" width="115" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">pom.xml's role</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-structure"><h3>📖 Worked example — reading a Spring Boot project tree</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — where each file lives and why</div><pre>src/main/java/com/example/demo/
  DemoApplication.java        ← @SpringBootApplication; root package
  controllers/CustomerController.java   ← web layer
  entities/Customer.java                ← JPA entities
  repositories/CustomerRepository.java  ← data layer
  services/CustomerService.java         ← business rules
src/main/resources/
  application.properties      ← configuration
  static/  templates/         ← front-end assets (if served)
pom.xml                       ← dependencies (starters)</pre>Component scanning starts at DemoApplication&rsquo;s package and descends — which is why every layer folder sits UNDER it. A controller placed outside that tree silently never registers (the &ldquo;my endpoint 404s&rdquo; bug).</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ LAYERS ARE A CONVENTION WITH TEETH</div>controller → service → repository, each calling only the next: graders and interviewers both look for it. A controller talking straight to the repository skips validation and business rules.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>A standard Spring Boot project follows a layered structure: entity, repository, service, controller. Following the convention makes code reviewable, code reviews predictable, and onboarding fast. Fighting the structure or flattening it leads to tangled dependencies.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. The standard package layout.</strong> com..app — main application class. com..app.entity — JPA entities. com..app.repository — Spring Data JPA repositories. com..app.service — business logic. com..app.controller — REST controllers. Optional: dto, config, exception, util.</p>
<p><strong>2. Why the layered structure.</strong> Each layer has a single responsibility. Controllers handle HTTP. Services handle business logic. Repositories handle persistence. Entities represent data. Layers depend downward — controllers depend on services, services on repositories, repositories on entities. Reverse dependencies are smells.</p>
<p><strong>3. The main application class.</strong> @SpringBootApplication on a class with main(). SpringApplication.run(MyApp.class, args) starts everything. The class's package is the root for component scanning — every @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller below it is auto-discovered.</p>
<p><strong>4. pom.xml's role.</strong> Maven's project file declares dependencies (the starters bring transitive jars), plugins (spring-boot-maven-plugin builds the executable JAR), and project metadata. You edit it to add or remove starters; the rest is set by parent inheritance.</p>
<p><strong>5. Starter dependencies bundle ecosystems.</strong> spring-boot-starter-web brings Spring MVC + embedded Tomcat + Jackson + validation. spring-boot-starter-data-jpa brings Hibernate + JPA + transaction management. mysql-connector-j brings the MySQL JDBC driver. Add the right starters for the features you need.</p>
<p><strong>6. src/main/resources contents.</strong> application.properties (config). static/ (CSS, JS, images served as-is). templates/ (server-rendered views — Thymeleaf, etc.). data.sql / schema.sql (auto-run SQL on startup, useful for seeding). Each subdirectory has Spring Boot conventions baked in.</p>
<p><strong>7. Test sources mirror main.</strong> src/test/java/com//app/... mirrors src/main/java. Each layer has tests in the matching test package. JUnit 5 is the default. @SpringBootTest spins up the application context for integration tests; standalone JUnit tests for unit tests.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Putting @Component classes outside the @SpringBootApplication's package — component scanning misses them.</li>
 <li>Flattening the layered structure into one big package — works small, becomes unreadable fast.</li>
 <li>Reverse dependencies (entity importing service) — design smell that will hurt later.</li>
 <li>Multiple main classes — Spring Boot finds the wrong one and starts unexpected code paths.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Standard structure: entity, repository, service, controller — layered with downward dependencies. Main class with @SpringBootApplication is the entry point and component scan root. pom.xml declares dependencies; starters bundle ecosystems. src/main/resources holds config and static content. Tests mirror the main package structure. Following the conventions makes everything else easier.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Layered architecture</span></dt>
 <dd>Pattern of separating code into entity, repository, service, controller layers.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@SpringBootApplication</span></dt>
 <dd>Main annotation marking the class with main(); composite of @Configuration + @EnableAutoConfiguration + @ComponentScan.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Component scan</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring's process of discovering @Component-annotated classes under the base package.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">pom.xml</span></dt>
 <dd>Maven build file declaring dependencies, plugins, and project metadata.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Starter</span></dt>
 <dd>Curated bundle of related Spring Boot dependencies.</dd>
</dl><p><strong>Maven project layout:</strong> production code lives in src/main/java; <strong>src/test/java is the test root</strong> — JUnit tests live there and run on every build. A sane <strong>.gitignore</strong> excludes build outputs and machine-local files: target/, build/, *.class, .idea/, .vscode/, and local application properties with secrets.</p>"},"D288/ch3/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="548" y2="97" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="461" y2="199" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="179" y2="199" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="92" y2="97" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Entity Classes &amp; JPA…</text><rect x="225" y="18" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@GeneratedValue for auto-IDs</text><rect x="476" y="82" width="144" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="548" y="101" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@Column for column</text><rect x="366" y="184" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="461" y="203" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Field types and conversion</text><rect x="100" y="184" width="158" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="179" y="203" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Lombok with entities</text><rect x="-3" y="82" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="92" y="101" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Naming conventions matter</text></svg></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Entities are the bridge between Java objects and database tables. Get the annotations right and JPA handles persistence almost invisibly. Get them wrong and you fight Hibernate exceptions for days. The mapping is small but precise; understanding each annotation pays back constantly.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. @Entity marks a persistent class.</strong> @Entity on a class tells JPA this represents a database table. By default, the table name matches the class name (Customer → CUSTOMER). Override with @Table(name = "customers"). The class needs a no-arg constructor (JPA's reflection requires it) and a primary key.</p>
<p><strong>2. @Id marks the primary key.</strong> Every entity needs exactly one @Id field. Without it, JPA fails at startup. The id is what JPA uses to identify and look up entities. Often a Long for auto-increment IDs; UUID for distributed-friendly identifiers.</p>
<p><strong>3. @GeneratedValue for auto-IDs.</strong> @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY) lets the database assign auto-increment IDs (typical for MySQL). GenerationType.SEQUENCE for sequence-based DBs (Oracle, Postgres). GenerationType.AUTO lets the provider pick. For MySQL on the PA, IDENTITY is correct.</p>
<p><strong>4. @Column for column-level details.</strong> @Column(name = "customer_name", length = 100, nullable = false) overrides the default column name and adds constraints. Many fields need no @Column at all — the defaults are sensible. Add it when overriding name, length, nullability, or uniqueness.</p>
<p><strong>5. Field types and conversion.</strong> Java types map to SQL types automatically: String → VARCHAR, int/Integer → INT, Long → BIGINT, BigDecimal → DECIMAL, LocalDate → DATE, LocalDateTime → DATETIME, Enum → VARCHAR (with @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)) or INT (default, dangerous). For enums, ALWAYS use @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING).</p>
<p><strong>6. Lombok with entities.</strong> @Data generates getters, setters, equals, hashCode, toString. @NoArgsConstructor and @AllArgsConstructor generate constructors. @Builder generates a fluent builder. Common pattern: @Entity @Data @NoArgsConstructor @AllArgsConstructor on top of an entity class. Saves dozens of lines per entity.</p>
<p><strong>7. Naming conventions matter.</strong> Database conventions (snake_case columns, plural table names) often don't match Java conventions (camelCase fields). Spring Boot's default naming strategy converts customerName → customer_name automatically. For exact matches with a pre-existing schema, use explicit @Column(name=...). The PA's schema may require this.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Missing no-arg constructor — JPA fails at startup with a cryptic 'no default constructor' error.</li>
 <li>@Enumerated default (ORDINAL) — adding a value mid-list reorders database mappings disastrously. Always use STRING.</li>
 <li>@GeneratedValue without specifying strategy — sometimes works, not portable.</li>
 <li>Forgetting to match column names to a pre-existing PA schema — SQL errors at runtime.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>@Entity marks a class as persistent. @Id picks the primary key. @GeneratedValue auto-generates it (IDENTITY for MySQL). @Column tunes column-level details. Java types map to SQL automatically; use @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) for enums. Lombok eliminates boilerplate. Match column names to the PA schema explicitly when defaults don't align.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Entity</span></dt>
 <dd>JPA annotation marking a class as a persistent entity.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Id</span></dt>
 <dd>Marks the field that serves as the entity's primary key.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@GeneratedValue</span></dt>
 <dd>Auto-generates the primary key; strategies include IDENTITY, SEQUENCE, AUTO.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Column</span></dt>
 <dd>Configures column-level details: name, length, nullability, uniqueness.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)</span></dt>
 <dd>Stores enum values as strings; safer than ordinal storage.</dd>
</dl>"},"D288/ch3/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="560" y2="125" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="80" y2="125" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Entity Relationships</text><rect x="280" y="18" width="80" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@OneToOne</text><rect x="506" y="110" width="108" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="560" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Cascade types</text><rect x="227" y="202" width="186" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">FetchType: LAZY vs EAGER</text><rect x="-3" y="110" width="165" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="80" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">The N+1 query problem</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-relations"><h3>📖 Worked example — mapping a one-to-many in JPA</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — Division ↔ Country style pairing</div><pre>@Entity
public class Country {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "country", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private Set&lt;Division&gt; divisions = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();
}

@Entity
public class Division {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "country_id")   // FK column lives on the MANY side
    private Country country;
}</pre>The division table carries country_id — the many side owns the foreign key; mappedBy on the one side points back at the owning field. Loading a Country can then walk country.getDivisions(); saving with cascade persists the whole family.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ mappedBy NAMES A FIELD, NOT A TABLE</div>mappedBy = "country" refers to the Java FIELD in Division. Typo it and you get a phantom join table and duplicate data. When a relationship &ldquo;doubles,&rdquo; check mappedBy first.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Real domain models have relationships: a Customer has Orders; an Order has LineItems; a Product has Categories. JPA relationship annotations express these in code, but the way they're configured affects performance, query behavior, and even whether deletes cascade correctly. Most production JPA bugs trace back to relationship missteps.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. @OneToMany and @ManyToOne are paired.</strong> A Customer has many Orders (@OneToMany); an Order has one Customer (@ManyToOne). The owning side (typically the @ManyToOne) holds the foreign key column. The inverse side uses mappedBy to point back: @OneToMany(mappedBy = "customer").</p>
<p><strong>2. @JoinColumn on the owning side.</strong> @ManyToOne @JoinColumn(name = "customer_id") Customer customer. This says: the orders table has a customer_id column referencing customers(id). If you don't specify @JoinColumn, JPA picks a default name; often you want explicit control matching a pre-existing schema.</p>
<p><strong>3. @OneToOne.</strong> Less common. Customer has one Address. Owning side has @JoinColumn; inverse uses mappedBy. Often modeled as embedded objects (@Embedded) instead when the relationship is truly 1:1 and the related data is conceptually 'part of' the entity.</p>
<p><strong>4. @ManyToMany requires a join table.</strong> Customer has many Products in their cart; products belong to many carts. Each side has @ManyToMany; one side names the join table with @JoinTable. The other side uses mappedBy. Common in the PA for cart-to-product or order-to-product associations.</p>
<p><strong>5. Cascade types.</strong> CascadeType.PERSIST means saving the parent saves the children. CascadeType.REMOVE deletes children when parent is deleted. CascadeType.ALL covers all of them. Be careful with REMOVE — accidental cascade can wipe data. Default is no cascade. Add deliberately, not by reflex.</p>
<p><strong>6. FetchType: LAZY vs EAGER.</strong> FetchType.LAZY loads associated entities on first access. FetchType.EAGER loads them immediately with the parent. @ManyToOne defaults to EAGER (often wrong). @OneToMany defaults to LAZY (usually right). Eager fetching is the leading cause of N+1 query problems.</p>
<p><strong>7. The N+1 query problem.</strong> Loading 100 customers and accessing each one's orders triggers 1 query for customers + 100 queries for orders. Fix with JPQL fetch joins (LEFT JOIN FETCH) or @EntityGraph. Profile your queries; this hides until it dominates response time.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Defaulting to @ManyToOne EAGER fetching everywhere — every parent load explodes into N+1 queries.</li>
 <li>CascadeType.ALL on relationships where children should outlive the parent — accidental data loss.</li>
 <li>Bidirectional @OneToMany where only one side is updated — data inconsistency until next reload.</li>
 <li>Forgetting mappedBy on the inverse side — JPA creates two relationships and a phantom join table.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>@OneToMany / @ManyToOne pair, with mappedBy on the inverse side. @ManyToMany uses @JoinTable. @JoinColumn names the foreign key. Cascade types control propagation; default to none. Fetch types control loading; lazy by default for performance. Watch for N+1 queries; use fetch joins when needed.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@OneToMany / @ManyToOne</span></dt>
 <dd>JPA annotations for one-to-many parent-child relationships.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ManyToMany</span></dt>
 <dd>JPA annotation for many-to-many relationships, requiring a join table.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">mappedBy</span></dt>
 <dd>Names the field on the owning side; declares this side as the inverse.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Cascade type</span></dt>
 <dd>Controls how operations (persist, remove, merge) propagate from parent to children.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">N+1 query problem</span></dt>
 <dd>Performance issue from loading children one at a time after loading the parent collection.</dd>
</dl>"},"D288/ch3/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Spring Data JPA…</text><rect x="241" y="18" width="158" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Extend JpaRepository</text><rect x="445" y="64" width="165" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Built-in CRUD methods</text><rect x="445" y="156" width="165" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Derived query methods</text><rect x="234" y="202" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@Query for custom JPQL</text><rect x="40" y="156" width="144" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">CrudRepository vs…</text><rect x="26" y="64" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Pagination and sorting</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-repos"><h3>📖 Worked example — a repository interface doing all the work</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — CRUD + derived query, no SQL written</div><pre>public interface CustomerRepository
        extends CrudRepository&lt;Customer, Long&gt; {
    List&lt;Customer&gt; findByLastName(String lastName);
}

// used in a service:
Customer saved = repo.save(new Customer("Amara", "Okafor"));
Optional&lt;Customer&gt; one = repo.findById(saved.getId());
List&lt;Customer&gt; okafors = repo.findByLastName("Okafor");
repo.deleteById(saved.getId());</pre>You wrote ONE method signature; Spring Data parsed findBy + LastName into <code>WHERE last_name = ?</code> and generated the implementation. save() inserts when id is null and updates when it exists — one method, both behaviors.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ findById RETURNS OPTIONAL</div>Handle absence explicitly: <code>repo.findById(id).orElseThrow(() -&gt; new NotFoundException(...))</code>. Calling .get() blind is the NoSuchElementException everyone hits once.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Spring Data JPA generates repository implementations from interface declarations. You write three lines; you get findById, save, delete, count, findAll, paging, sorting — the entire CRUD layer. Knowing how to use it (and what it generates) lets you skip thousands of lines of boilerplate.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Extend JpaRepository.</strong> interface CustomerRepository extends JpaRepository&lt;Customer, Long. Two type parameters: the entity type and the primary-key type. Spring creates an implementation at startup. You inject the interface; the magic is invisible.</p>
<p><strong>2. Built-in CRUD methods.</strong> save(entity) — insert or update. findById(id) — Optional&lt;Customer. findAll() — List&lt;Customer. delete(entity), deleteById(id), count(), existsById(id). Plus paging and sorting variants. You get all of these from one interface declaration.</p>
<p><strong>3. Derived query methods.</strong> Method names parsed into queries: findByEmail(String email), findByLastNameContaining(String fragment), findByCreatedAtAfter(LocalDateTime t). The naming convention is a small DSL — verb + property + operator. Most CRUD apps don't need anything else.</p>
<p><strong>4. @Query for custom JPQL.</strong> When derived names get long or queries get complex, write JPQL: @Query("SELECT c FROM Customer c WHERE c.totalSpent > :min"). JPQL operates on entities, not tables. Native SQL is also available: @Query(value = "SELECT * FROM customers WHERE ...", nativeQuery = true).</p>
<p><strong>5. CrudRepository vs JpaRepository.</strong> CrudRepository: basic CRUD. PagingAndSortingRepository: adds paging. JpaRepository: adds JPA-specific features (flush, batch save, getReferenceById). For most applications, JpaRepository is the right choice — extends both ancestors.</p>
<p><strong>6. Pagination and sorting.</strong> findAll(Pageable pageable) returns Page&lt;Customer. PageRequest.of(0, 10, Sort.by("name")) requests the first 10 sorted by name. Page&lt;T exposes content, total elements, total pages, page number. Pagination is built in; you don't write a single line of SQL.</p>
<p><strong>7. Service layer above repositories.</strong> Repository methods stay simple — single-purpose CRUD and queries. Put business logic (validating, calculating, sending notifications) in @Service classes that use repositories. Keeping repositories thin keeps test mocks simple and the service layer cohesive.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Long derived method names that are harder to read than custom @Query — switch when names exceed 5 keywords.</li>
 <li>Calling findAll() on huge tables and iterating in memory — use Pageable to fetch in chunks.</li>
 <li>Putting business logic in repositories — they should only know about persistence.</li>
 <li>Native SQL when JPQL would do — locks you to one database vendor.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Extend JpaRepository&lt;Entity, Id and get full CRUD for free. Derived query methods follow a simple naming DSL. @Query handles complex cases with JPQL or native SQL. Pagination and sorting are built in. Keep repositories thin; put business logic in services. The boilerplate-eliminating power of Spring Data is enormous; lean into it.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">JpaRepository</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Data interface providing CRUD, paging, and sorting for an entity type.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Derived query method</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Data convention parsing method names into queries (findByEmail, findByPriceLessThan).</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Query</span></dt>
 <dd>Custom JPQL or native-SQL query attached to a repository method.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Pageable</span></dt>
 <dd>Argument carrying page number, size, and sort; returns a Page&lt;Entity.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">JPQL</span></dt>
 <dd>Java Persistence Query Language — entity-aware query syntax similar to SQL but operating on entities.</dd>
</dl>"},"D288/ch3/s4":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">PA Entity Modeling</text><rect x="234" y="18" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Customer at the center</text><rect x="445" y="64" width="165" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Product and Inventory</text><rect x="452" y="156" width="151" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Cart and Cart Items</text><rect x="237" y="202" width="165" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Order and Order Items</text><rect x="19" y="156" width="186" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Cascade and fetch tuning</text><rect x="37" y="64" width="151" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Money is BigDecimal</text></svg></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>The D288 Performance Assessment provides an ERD (entity-relationship diagram) and expects you to model it in JPA entities. Getting the entities right — class names, columns, relationships, cascade settings — is the foundation of every other PA task. Misunderstand the ERD and every later task fights it.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Read the ERD before writing entities.</strong> The ERD shows tables, columns, primary keys, foreign keys, relationships. Each table maps to one entity class. Each column maps to one field. Each relationship maps to one of @OneToOne, @OneToMany, @ManyToOne, @ManyToMany. Map by hand on paper before opening the IDE.</p>
<p><strong>2. Customer at the center.</strong> Customer is the user. Has fields like first_name, last_name, address, phone, email, postal_code, division_id (foreign key to Division). Relates to Division (@ManyToOne — many customers in one division), and to Order/Cart depending on the PA's specifics.</p>
<p><strong>3. Division and Country — geography.</strong> Division (state or province) belongs to a Country. @ManyToOne from Division to Country. Customer references Division. The geography hierarchy supports tax rules and shipping logic. Often pre-populated by data.sql in the PA.</p>
<p><strong>4. Product and Inventory.</strong> Product has SKU, name, price, image, etc. Inventory tracks stock count separately (sometimes embedded in Product, sometimes its own entity). Read the ERD carefully — small structural differences (separate tables vs combined fields) change the JPA mapping.</p>
<p><strong>5. Cart and Cart Items.</strong> Cart is associated with one Customer (@OneToOne or @ManyToOne depending on whether multiple carts per customer). CartItem links Cart to Product with a quantity. Often modeled as @ManyToMany with extra fields, which becomes its own @Entity (CartItem) joining the two.</p>
<p><strong>6. Order and Order Items.</strong> After checkout, the cart converts to an Order. Order has order_date, total, status, customer_id. OrderItem links Order to Product with quantity and price-at-time-of-purchase (so historical orders show the right prices even if products change).</p>
<p><strong>7. Cascade and fetch tuning.</strong> Customer → Cart often uses CascadeType.ALL with orphanRemoval=true (deleting a customer deletes their cart). Cart → CartItem similarly. Order → OrderItem cascade ALL since order items don't exist independent of orders. Fetch types: usually LAZY for collections, EAGER for parents you almost always need.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Skipping the ERD analysis and starting to code — entities don't match, relationships break, hours wasted.</li>
 <li>Wrong cascade settings — deleting a customer either fails (no cascade) or wipes orders (cascade ALL).</li>
 <li>Missing fields the schema requires — runtime SQL errors when the column is NOT NULL but the entity lacks it.</li>
 <li>Mismatched column names — JPA emits queries that don't match the schema; everything looks broken.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>The PA gives you an ERD; map it carefully to entities. Customer at the center. Division/Country supports geography. Product, Cart, CartItem, Order, OrderItem form the e-commerce data model. Get cascade and fetch settings deliberate. Match column names to the schema. Start with paper; the IDE comes later.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">ERD (Entity-Relationship Diagram)</span></dt>
 <dd>Visual model of database tables, columns, keys, and relationships.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Customer entity</span></dt>
 <dd>Central entity representing a user with personal and address details.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Division</span></dt>
 <dd>Geographical subdivision (state, province) referenced by Customer.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Cart / CartItem</span></dt>
 <dd>Pre-checkout selection of products; CartItem is the join with quantity.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Order / OrderItem</span></dt>
 <dd>Post-checkout record of purchased products with historical pricing.</dd>
</dl><p><strong>Money is BigDecimal</strong>, never double — binary floating point cannot represent 0.10 exactly and pennies drift. <strong>Order totals:</strong> either store the total (denormalized — fast reads, must keep in sync) or compute it from the items every time (always consistent, costs a little per read). Know the tradeoff; both are defensible when stated.</p>"},"D288/ch4/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-service"><h3>📖 Worked example — why the service layer exists</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a purchase that touches two tables</div><pre>@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository orders;
    private final ProductRepository products;
    public OrderService(OrderRepository o, ProductRepository p) {
        this.orders = o; this.products = p;
    }

    @Transactional
    public Order placeOrder(Long productId, int qty) {
        Product prod = products.findById(productId).orElseThrow();
        if (prod.getStock() &lt; qty)
            throw new IllegalStateException("Insufficient stock");
        prod.setStock(prod.getStock() - qty);   // change 1
        return orders.save(new Order(prod, qty)); // change 2 — same transaction
    }
}</pre>The business rule (stock check) and the two coordinated writes live HERE — not in the controller (which just maps HTTP) and not in the repository (which just persists). @Transactional makes the pair atomic: fail anywhere, both roll back.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ CONTROLLERS STAY THIN</div>If a controller method contains an if about stock or prices, the design aspect fails. Controller = translate HTTP; service = decide; repository = store.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>The service layer is where business logic lives. Without it, controllers become bloated and repositories absorb logic that doesn't belong there. With it, each layer has a clean responsibility and the code is testable, evolvable, and reviewable.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. What goes in services.</strong> Business rules: 'a customer can't checkout with an empty cart.' Calculations: total + tax. Multi-step operations: create order + decrement inventory + send confirmation. Cross-cutting concerns: validation, authorization, audit logging. Anything that's not pure CRUD or pure HTTP.</p>
<p><strong>2. What does NOT go in services.</strong> Database access (that's repositories). HTTP details — request parameters, response codes, headers (that's controllers). Entity-internal logic (that's entities or domain methods). Service code reads naturally as 'do this business operation.'</p>
<p><strong>3. @Service marks a service class.</strong> @Service public class CheckoutService. Spring registers it as a bean. Inject repositories via constructor. Stateless (singleton scope) — each method invocation is independent. Multiple services can collaborate; one service often uses several repositories.</p>
<p><strong>4. Constructor injection of repositories.</strong> private final CartRepository cartRepo; public CheckoutService(CartRepository cartRepo) { this.cartRepo = cartRepo; }. Lombok's @RequiredArgsConstructor generates this. The service doesn't create or know how repos are constructed — IoC takes care of it.</p>
<p><strong>5. @Transactional for atomic operations.</strong> Methods that touch multiple entities or perform multi-step operations should be transactional. @Transactional on the method (or class) wraps it in a database transaction — either everything commits or nothing does. Default rollback on RuntimeException.</p>
<p><strong>6. Service interfaces — when to use.</strong> Some teams define service interfaces (CheckoutService) and concrete classes (CheckoutServiceImpl). Useful when you anticipate multiple implementations or want a strict API boundary. For most apps, the concrete class is enough. The PA likely doesn't require interfaces; don't add them gratuitously.</p>
<p><strong>7. Single-responsibility services.</strong> One service per domain area: CustomerService, ProductService, CheckoutService. Don't create one giant service with 30 methods. If a service is doing too much, split. If two services need to call each other, that's usually fine — Spring handles it.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Putting business logic in controllers — bloated controllers, untestable logic.</li>
 <li>Forgetting @Transactional on multi-step operations — partial commits leave data inconsistent.</li>
 <li>Calling another @Transactional method from the same class — Spring's proxy doesn't intercept; the @Transactional doesn't apply.</li>
 <li>Service interfaces nobody needs — adds a layer of abstraction without benefit.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Service layer holds business logic — rules, calculations, multi-step operations. Mark with @Service. Inject repositories via constructor. Use @Transactional for multi-step database operations. One service per domain area. Avoid interfaces unless you genuinely need them. Keep controllers thin and repositories thin; the service is where the work lives.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Service layer</span></dt>
 <dd>Layer of classes containing business logic, sitting between controllers and repositories.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Service</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring annotation marking a class as a business-logic bean.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Transactional</span></dt>
 <dd>Annotation wrapping a method in a database transaction with auto-rollback on RuntimeException.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Single Responsibility Principle</span></dt>
 <dd>Each class (or service) should have one focused responsibility.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Constructor injection</span></dt>
 <dd>Pattern of receiving dependencies as constructor parameters and storing as final fields.</dd>
</dl>"},"D288/ch4/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-validate"><h3>📖 Worked example — Bean Validation on an incoming request</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — reject bad input before it reaches the service</div><pre>public class CustomerRequest {
    @NotBlank(message = "Name is required")
    private String name;

    @Email(message = "Valid email required")
    private String email;

    @Min(value = 0, message = "Points cannot be negative")
    private int points;
}

@PostMapping("/customers")
public ResponseEntity&lt;?&gt; create(@Valid @RequestBody CustomerRequest req) {
    ...   // only reached when ALL rules pass
}</pre>POST {"name":"", "email":"nope", "points":-5} → Spring runs the annotations, collects all three failures, and responds <b>400 Bad Request</b> with the messages — your service never sees garbage. @Valid on the parameter is the trigger; forget it and the annotations silently do nothing.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ @NotBlank vs @NotNull vs @NotEmpty</div>@NotNull allows &nbsp;""&nbsp;; @NotEmpty allows &nbsp;"&nbsp;&nbsp;"&nbsp;; <b>@NotBlank</b> requires actual visible content — for names and required text, NotBlank is almost always the right pick.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Validation prevents bad data from reaching your business logic and your database. Bean Validation provides annotation-driven rules that run automatically. Used well, your service methods stay clean and your API consumers get clear error messages for bad inputs.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Annotations on entity / DTO fields.</strong> @NotNull (must not be null). @NotBlank (string must contain non-whitespace). @Size(min, max) (string or collection length). @Min, @Max (numeric bounds). @Email (valid email pattern). @Pattern (regex). Apply directly to fields and they become contracts.</p>
<p><strong>2. @Valid triggers validation.</strong> @PostMapping("/customers") public ResponseEntity&lt;Customer create(@Valid @RequestBody Customer customer). Without @Valid, the annotations are decoration only — Spring won't check them. With @Valid, validation runs and errors propagate as MethodArgumentNotValidException.</p>
<p><strong>3. Customer-specific constraints from the PA.</strong> @NotBlank on first_name and last_name. @Email on email. @Pattern on postal_code matching the expected format. @Size on phone matching the length. The PA's rubric specifies which rules; encode each as the right annotation.</p>
<p><strong>4. Custom error messages.</strong> @NotBlank(message = "Customer name is required"). @Email(message = "Email format is invalid"). Defaults are generic ('must not be blank'); rubric-matching messages reduce friction with graders and improve UX. Include the message text on each annotation.</p>
<p><strong>5. Handling validation errors globally.</strong> @RestControllerAdvice with @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class) catches every validation failure. Return a structured JSON error response: { errors: [{ field: 'email', message: '...' }, ...] }. Consistent error format across endpoints.</p>
<p><strong>6. Cross-field validation.</strong> When a rule depends on multiple fields (e.g., 'shipDate must be after orderDate'), field-level annotations don't suffice. Implement a custom annotation + ConstraintValidator. Or check programmatically in the service. For most PA rules, field-level is enough.</p>
<p><strong>7. Validation in services vs controllers.</strong> Validation at the controller boundary catches bad inputs before they reach business logic. Validation in services catches issues that depend on database state (e.g., 'cart cannot exceed inventory'). Use both; they catch different things.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Forgetting @Valid — validation annotations sit unused.</li>
 <li>Hard-coded default messages — generic 'must not be blank' is less useful than 'Customer name is required.'</li>
 <li>Mixing validation logic in controllers AND services without coordination — inconsistent error responses.</li>
 <li>No global exception handler — validation failures return 500 errors instead of 400 with details.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Annotate fields with @NotNull, @NotBlank, @Size, @Min, @Max, @Email, @Pattern. Trigger with @Valid in controllers. Customize messages to match rubric language. Handle MethodArgumentNotValidException globally with @RestControllerAdvice. Validate at the boundary AND in services where database state matters. Cover each PA-specified rule with the right annotation.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Bean Validation (JSR-380)</span></dt>
 <dd>Java standard for annotation-driven validation.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Valid</span></dt>
 <dd>Triggers validation of the annotated parameter.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">MethodArgumentNotValidException</span></dt>
 <dd>Exception Spring throws when @Valid validation fails.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@RestControllerAdvice</span></dt>
 <dd>Class-level annotation collecting global exception handlers and other cross-cutting concerns.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">ConstraintValidator</span></dt>
 <dd>Interface implemented for custom validation logic beyond field-level annotations.</dd>
</dl><i data-wgub2_D288_ch4_s2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — identifiers in the checkout flow</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk467a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">UUID</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">random 128-bit unique identifier</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">why not the PK</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">sequential ids are guessable and leak volume</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">independent of the DB</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">generated before the row is written</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Java</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">UUID.randomUUID().toString()</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">collision risk</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">negligible for practical purposes</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">use for</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">order tracking numbers, public-facing references</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — A tracking number that reveals nothing</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5; margin:10px 0;">import java.util.UUID;

String tracking = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
System.out.println("length: " + tracking.length());
System.out.println("hyphen groups: " + tracking.split("-").length);
System.out.println("two calls differ: " +
    !UUID.randomUUID().toString().equals(UUID.randomUUID().toString()));
System.out.println("version digit: " + tracking.charAt(14));</pre><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); background:var(--surface-2); padding:8px 12px; margin:8px 0 4px; font-family:ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; white-space:pre-wrap;">length: 36
hyphen groups: 5
two calls differ: true
version digit: 4</div><div style="font-size:11px; color:var(--text-3); font-style:italic; margin-bottom:10px;">actual output, captured from the JVM</div><ol><li>A UUID string is 36 characters — 32 hex digits in five hyphen-separated groups.</li><li>Two calls never realistically collide, so no coordination with the database is needed to guarantee uniqueness.</li><li>The version digit is 4, marking a random (v4) UUID. Because it carries no sequence, a customer cannot guess another order's number or infer how many orders exist.</li></ol></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>For an order-tracking number in the D288 checkout flow, use a <strong>UUID</strong>. The reason is not just uniqueness — a sequential database id would let a customer guess neighbouring orders and read your order volume off the number.</div>"},"D288/ch4/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">PA Checkout Service…</text><rect x="252" y="18" width="137" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">The checkout flow</text><rect x="456" y="64" width="144" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Calculating totals</text><rect x="449" y="156" width="158" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Creating order items</text><rect x="225" y="202" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Atomicity — @Transactional</text><rect x="17" y="156" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Returning the right response</text><rect x="51" y="64" width="122" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">CheckoutService</text></svg></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>The checkout service is the heart of the e-commerce PA. It receives a customer's cart and produces an order — calculating totals, creating order items, persisting everything, returning the result. It's where business rules meet persistence and where most of the rubric weight sits.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. The checkout flow.</strong> Receive request: cart ID or cart contents + customer. Validate: cart not empty, products in stock. Calculate: subtotal, tax, total. Create Order entity. Create OrderItem entities for each cart item. Persist everything (@Transactional). Optionally clear the cart. Return the Order.</p>
<p><strong>2. Calculating totals.</strong> Sum (price × quantity) for each cart item → subtotal. Apply tax based on customer's division — usually a tax rate from a Division or Country entity. tax = subtotal × taxRate. total = subtotal + tax. Use BigDecimal for money — never double or float (precision errors).</p>
<p><strong>3. Tax calculation depends on geography.</strong> Division has a tax rate (or references Country which does). Look up tax via the Customer's Division. PA likely provides specific tax rules per division (e.g., NYC has a specific rate). Encode the rules from the PA spec; don't hard-code 'all customers pay 8%.'</p>
<p><strong>4. Creating order items.</strong> For each cart item, instantiate a new OrderItem with: order reference, product reference, quantity, price-at-time-of-checkout. The price-at-checkout matters: even if a product's price changes later, the historical order shows what was paid. Don't reuse the live Product price for past orders.</p>
<p><strong>5. Atomicity — @Transactional.</strong> The whole checkout must be atomic. Either the order is created, items inserted, cart updated, inventory decremented all together, OR nothing happens. @Transactional on the checkout method ensures this. A failure mid-way rolls back everything.</p>
<p><strong>6. Edge cases the PA tests.</strong> Empty cart — return 400 with message. Out-of-stock product — return 400 or specific status. Non-existent customer — return 404. Each case should be handled with a clear exception that the global exception handler converts to an appropriate HTTP response.</p>
<p><strong>7. Returning the right response.</strong> Typical response: the created Order (with its ID, items, totals). Status 201 Created. Location header pointing to /orders/{id}. The Angular front-end renders the response; making it correct unblocks UI testing.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Using double or float for money calculations — precision errors compound; use BigDecimal.</li>
 <li>Storing live Product price in OrderItem instead of price-at-checkout — historical orders become wrong.</li>
 <li>Not @Transactional — partial failures leave the database inconsistent.</li>
 <li>Hard-coded tax rate — PA expects rates derived from division/country.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Checkout: receive cart, validate, calculate totals (subtotal + tax based on customer's division), create Order and OrderItems, persist atomically (@Transactional), return the Order. Use BigDecimal for money. Store price-at-checkout in OrderItem. Handle edge cases (empty cart, out of stock, missing customer) with appropriate exceptions and HTTP status codes.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Checkout</span></dt>
 <dd>Process of converting a customer's cart into a persistent Order.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">BigDecimal</span></dt>
 <dd>Exact decimal arithmetic class; required for money to avoid floating-point errors.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Order item</span></dt>
 <dd>Line item in an order, holding product reference, quantity, and price-at-checkout.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Tax rate</span></dt>
 <dd>Percentage applied to subtotal, typically derived from customer's geography.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Atomic transaction</span></dt>
 <dd>Operation that either fully succeeds or fully reverts; provided by @Transactional.</dd>
</dl><p><strong>Cascading saves:</strong> with cascade = ALL on Order’s items relationship, saving the Order persists its items in one operation — the parent owns the children’s lifecycle. A clean <strong>CheckoutService</strong> API reads like the business: placeOrder, cancelOrder, getOrderById, getOrdersForCustomer — verbs for commands, get* for queries.</p>"},"D288/ch5/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">REST Architecture &amp; HTTP…</text><rect x="255" y="18" width="130" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">nouns in the URL</text><rect x="488" y="64" width="80" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">HTTP verb</text><rect x="467" y="156" width="122" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">POST — create,…</text><rect x="225" y="202" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">PUT — replace, idempotent</text><rect x="26" y="156" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">PATCH — partial update</text><rect x="17" y="64" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">DELETE — remove, idempotent</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-rest"><h3>📖 Worked example — REST verbs mapped to one resource</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — /customers, the full verb table</div><pre>GET    /customers        → list all           (200 OK)
GET    /customers/7      → fetch one           (200, or 404 if absent)
POST   /customers        → create from body    (201 Created)
PUT    /customers/7      → replace/update #7   (200)
DELETE /customers/7      → remove #7           (204 No Content)</pre>REST = resources named by <b>nouns in the URL</b>, actions chosen by the <b>HTTP verb</b>, state carried as JSON. GET is safe (no changes) and repeatable; PUT and DELETE are idempotent (same call twice = same result); POST is the one that creates something new each time.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ STATUS CODES ARE PART OF THE CONTRACT</div>200 OK, 201 Created (with the new resource), 400 bad input, 404 missing resource, 500 server bug. Returning 200 for a failed lookup is a graded mistake — the code IS the message.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>REST is the architectural style behind almost every modern web API, including the PA's. Knowing the principles — resources, HTTP methods, statelessness, status codes — turns the API design from arbitrary to predictable. A well-designed API uses HTTP correctly; a poorly designed one fights it.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Resources, not actions.</strong> REST URLs identify resources (nouns), not actions. /customers, /products, /orders — yes. /createCustomer, /getOrder — no. Actions come from HTTP methods applied to resources. /customers/42 represents customer 42; what you do with them is determined by the verb.</p>
<p><strong>2. GET — retrieve, idempotent, cacheable.</strong> GET /customers — list all. GET /customers/42 — fetch one. Should never modify state. Calling it twice should yield the same result. Browsers cache GET responses; reverse proxies do too.</p>
<p><strong>3. POST — create, non-idempotent.</strong> POST /customers with body — create a new customer. The server assigns the ID. Calling twice creates two customers. Returns 201 Created with Location header pointing to the new resource.</p>
<p><strong>4. PUT — replace, idempotent.</strong> PUT /customers/42 with full body — replace customer 42 entirely. Calling twice with the same body has the same end state. Use for full updates. Note: PUT replaces; missing fields become null.</p>
<p><strong>5. PATCH — partial update.</strong> PATCH /customers/42 with partial body — update specified fields only. Useful when you don't want to send the entire object. Less universally supported than PUT but increasingly standard.</p>
<p><strong>6. DELETE — remove, idempotent.</strong> DELETE /customers/42 — remove customer 42. Calling twice is a no-op the second time (resource already gone). Returns 204 No Content typically, or 200 with the deleted resource.</p>
<p><strong>7. HTTP status codes — be precise.</strong> 200 OK (general success). 201 Created (after POST). 204 No Content (after DELETE). 400 Bad Request (validation error). 401 Unauthorized (no/invalid auth). 403 Forbidden (auth but not allowed). 404 Not Found. 409 Conflict (duplicate). 500 Internal Server Error. Each has a standard meaning; using them correctly makes the API self-documenting.</p>
<p><strong>8. Statelessness.</strong> Each request contains everything the server needs. The server doesn't keep session state between requests for the same client — auth tokens, request bodies, query params carry the context. Statelessness enables horizontal scaling and simplifies failover.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>URLs with verbs (/createCustomer) — fights REST conventions, harder to use.</li>
 <li>Returning 200 OK for everything — clients can't distinguish errors from success.</li>
 <li>Using POST for things that should be GET — defeats caching, no longer idempotent.</li>
 <li>Stateful APIs with server-side sessions — breaks horizontal scaling, complicates testing.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>REST identifies resources with URLs and operates on them with HTTP methods. GET retrieves; POST creates; PUT replaces; PATCH partial-updates; DELETE removes. Each has expected idempotency and caching semantics. HTTP status codes have standard meanings — use them precisely. APIs are stateless: each request is self-contained.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">REST</span></dt>
 <dd>Representational State Transfer; architectural style using HTTP methods on resource URLs.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Idempotent</span></dt>
 <dd>Operation whose effect is the same whether performed once or many times (GET, PUT, DELETE).</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">HTTP method</span></dt>
 <dd>Verb (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) determining the action on a resource URL.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">HTTP status code</span></dt>
 <dd>Three-digit code communicating success, redirect, client error, or server error.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Statelessness</span></dt>
 <dd>Property that each request contains all information needed to process it.</dd>
</dl>"},"D288/ch5/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">@RestController &amp;…</text><rect x="245" y="18" width="151" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@RestController vs…</text><rect x="433" y="64" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@RequestMapping at class…</text><rect x="445" y="156" width="165" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">HTTP-method shortcuts</text><rect x="234" y="202" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@PathVariable for URL…</text><rect x="19" y="156" width="186" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@RequestParam for query…</text><rect x="17" y="64" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@RequestBody for JSON input</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-restcontroller"><h3>📖 Worked example — a complete @RestController</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — endpoints that return JSON</div><pre>@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/customers")
public class CustomerController {
    private final CustomerService service;
    public CustomerController(CustomerService s) { this.service = s; }

    @GetMapping                       // GET /api/customers
    public List&lt;Customer&gt; all() { return service.findAll(); }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")              // GET /api/customers/7
    public Customer one(@PathVariable Long id) { return service.find(id); }

    @PostMapping                      // POST /api/customers
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    public Customer create(@Valid @RequestBody Customer c) {
        return service.save(c);
    }
}</pre>@RestController = @Controller + @ResponseBody: return values are serialized to JSON automatically. @RequestMapping at the top prefixes every method&rsquo;s path. @PathVariable pulls {id} from the URL; @RequestBody builds the object from the JSON body.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ @Controller vs @RestController</div>@Controller returns VIEW NAMES (Thymeleaf pages); @RestController returns DATA. Use the wrong one and you get a 404-ish template error instead of JSON — a favorite what-went-wrong question.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Spring MVC's REST controller annotations map HTTP requests to Java methods. Get the annotations right and your API matches the design. Get them wrong — wrong path, wrong method, wrong parameter binding — and clients get errors that look like server bugs.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. @RestController vs @Controller.</strong> @RestController returns objects that get serialized to JSON. @Controller returns view names that get rendered with templates (Thymeleaf, JSP). For the PA's REST API, always @RestController. @RestController = @Controller + @ResponseBody on every method.</p>
<p><strong>2. @RequestMapping at class level.</strong> @RequestMapping("/customers") on a controller class sets the base path. All methods in the class are relative to /customers. Cleaner than repeating /customers in each method's mapping.</p>
<p><strong>3. HTTP-method shortcuts.</strong> @GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @PatchMapping, @DeleteMapping. Cleaner than @RequestMapping(method = ...). Each takes a path: @GetMapping("/{id}") on a class @RequestMapping("/customers") maps GET /customers/{id}.</p>
<p><strong>4. @PathVariable for URL segments.</strong> @GetMapping("/{id}") public Customer get(@PathVariable Long id). The {id} placeholder in the URL maps to the id parameter. Type conversion is automatic — Spring parses /customers/42 into Long 42. Mismatched types throw 400.</p>
<p><strong>5. @RequestParam for query strings.</strong> @GetMapping public List&lt;Customer search(@RequestParam(required=false) String email). /customers?email=foo@bar maps email to the parameter. required=false makes it optional with default null.</p>
<p><strong>6. @RequestBody for JSON input.</strong> @PostMapping public Customer create(@RequestBody Customer customer). Spring deserializes the request body's JSON into a Customer instance using Jackson. Combined with @Valid, you get validation for free.</p>
<p><strong>7. Returning JSON automatically.</strong> Since @RestController applies @ResponseBody to every method, the returned object is serialized via Jackson. Customer goes out as { "id": 42, "name": "Alice", ... }. No manual JSON building.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Using @Controller instead of @RestController — Spring tries to resolve view names, returns 500.</li>
 <li>Mismatched @PathVariable name and parameter — Spring binds wrong, debugger shows null.</li>
 <li>Forgetting @RequestBody on POST/PUT methods — Spring tries to parse fields from query string, fails silently.</li>
 <li>Returning entities directly without considering serialization — circular references in @OneToMany blow up Jackson.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>@RestController serializes returned objects to JSON. @RequestMapping at class level sets base path. @GetMapping/@PostMapping/etc. shortcut HTTP methods. @PathVariable binds URL segments. @RequestParam binds query strings. @RequestBody deserializes JSON input. Each piece has a clear role; misusing them causes 400/500 errors. Lean on Spring; don't fight it.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@RestController</span></dt>
 <dd>Combines @Controller and @ResponseBody — every method returns serialized JSON.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@RequestMapping</span></dt>
 <dd>Maps HTTP requests to handler methods; class-level sets a base path.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@PathVariable</span></dt>
 <dd>Annotation binding a URL path segment to a method parameter.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@RequestParam</span></dt>
 <dd>Annotation binding a query string parameter or form field to a method parameter.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@RequestBody</span></dt>
 <dd>Annotation deserializing the request body into a Java object via Jackson.</dd>
</dl><p><strong>ResponseEntity</strong> gives full control of the HTTP response — status code, headers, and body. Return <strong>ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(saved)</strong> for a creation endpoint (201, not 200). <strong>@RequestHeader</strong> binds an incoming REQUEST header to a parameter — it reads headers; to SET response headers you use ResponseEntity (or HttpServletResponse), not @RequestHeader.</p>"},"D288/ch5/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="548" y2="97" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="461" y2="199" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="179" y2="199" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="92" y2="97" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Response Handling &amp; Error…</text><rect x="225" y="18" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Returning entities directly</text><rect x="473" y="82" width="151" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="548" y="101" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@ResponseStatus on…</text><rect x="371" y="184" width="179" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="461" y="203" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@ExceptionHandler in a…</text><rect x="84" y="184" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="179" y="203" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Structured error responses</text><rect x="-3" y="82" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="92" y="101" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">The PA's specific responses</text></svg></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>What you return — status code, body, headers — determines how clients interpret your response. Returning the right ResponseEntity for each case (success, validation error, not found, server error) makes the API self-documenting and the front-end's job trivial.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. ResponseEntity for explicit control.</strong> ResponseEntity&lt;Customer wraps the response with body, status code, and headers. ResponseEntity.ok(customer) — 200 with body. ResponseEntity.status(201).body(customer) — Created. ResponseEntity.notFound().build() — 404 no body. Use when you need control beyond the default 200.</p>
<p><strong>2. Returning entities directly.</strong> @GetMapping public Customer get() returns Customer with default 200 OK. Spring serializes via Jackson. Cleanest for the success case where 200 is right and no extra headers are needed.</p>
<p><strong>3. @ResponseStatus on exceptions.</strong> Define exceptions like CustomerNotFoundException with @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND). When thrown, Spring auto-converts to a 404 response. Combined with @ExceptionHandler, you get clean exception-to-status mappings.</p>
<p><strong>4. @ExceptionHandler in a controller.</strong> @ExceptionHandler(CustomerNotFoundException.class) public ResponseEntity&lt;ErrorResponse handle(CustomerNotFoundException e). Catches the exception when thrown by handler methods in the same controller. Returns a clean error response.</p>
<p><strong>5. @RestControllerAdvice for global handling.</strong> An @RestControllerAdvice class collects @ExceptionHandlers that apply across all controllers. Avoids repeating handlers in each controller. Useful for: every NotFoundException → 404 with structured body; every validation error → 400 with field errors; every uncaught Exception → 500 with generic message.</p>
<p><strong>6. Structured error responses.</strong> Don't return strings. Return JSON: { "timestamp": "...", "status": 404, "error": "Not Found", "message": "Customer 42 not found", "path": "/customers/42" }. Consistent format across endpoints. Front-ends can handle errors uniformly.</p>
<p><strong>7. The PA's specific responses.</strong> Different rubric tasks expect different status codes. Read the rubric. Customer not found → 404. Validation error → 400 with field details. Successful POST → 201. Successful DELETE → 204. Match what the rubric asks for, not what feels right.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Returning 200 OK for errors — clients have no way to detect failure.</li>
 <li>Throwing generic RuntimeException without @ResponseStatus — propagates as 500.</li>
 <li>Inconsistent error response formats across endpoints — front-end has to handle each differently.</li>
 <li>Catching Exception broadly and swallowing — silent failures multiply over time.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Use ResponseEntity for explicit control over status and headers; return entity directly for default 200. @ResponseStatus on exception classes maps them to status codes. @ExceptionHandler (in controller or @RestControllerAdvice) converts exceptions to responses. Structured error JSON is consistent and useful. Match the PA's expected status codes from the rubric.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">ResponseEntity</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring class wrapping response body, status code, and headers.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ExceptionHandler</span></dt>
 <dd>Method-level annotation routing exceptions to a handler method.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@RestControllerAdvice</span></dt>
 <dd>Class-level annotation collecting global exception handlers for REST controllers.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ResponseStatus</span></dt>
 <dd>Sets the HTTP status code returned when an annotated exception propagates.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Structured error response</span></dt>
 <dd>Consistent JSON error format with status, message, timestamp, and path.</dd>
</dl>"},"D288/ch5/s4":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-parest"><h3>📖 Worked example — building one PA endpoint end to end</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — &ldquo;add a purchase endpoint that updates inventory&rdquo;</div>Requirement to running code, layer by layer:<br><pre>// 1. Controller — accept the HTTP call
@PostMapping("/api/orders/{productId}")
public ResponseEntity&lt;Order&gt; buy(@PathVariable Long productId,
                                @RequestParam int qty) {
    return ResponseEntity.status(201).body(service.placeOrder(productId, qty));
}

// 2. Service — the rules + transaction (stock check, decrement, save)
// 3. Repository — already generated by Spring Data
// 4. Verify in Postman: POST http://localhost:8080/api/orders/3?qty=2
//    → 201 + JSON body; then GET the product and CHECK stock dropped.</pre>Each PA endpoint is this same vertical slice: route in the controller, rule in the service, persistence via the repository, proof in Postman. Build one slice completely before starting the next.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ PROVE IT, DON&rsquo;T ASSUME IT</div>The requirement isn&rsquo;t done when the code compiles — it&rsquo;s done when the Postman round-trip shows the status code AND the database state change. Screenshot both for the submission.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>The PA's REST endpoints are the contract between the Spring Boot back end and the Angular front end. The list of endpoints, their paths, methods, request/response shapes, and status codes are specified in the PA. Implementing them correctly unblocks every UI test and rubric verification.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Customer endpoints.</strong> GET /api/customers — list all. GET /api/customers/{id} — fetch one. POST /api/customers — create new. PUT /api/customers/{id} — update existing. DELETE /api/customers/{id} — remove. Standard REST resource set; each maps to a CustomerController method.</p>
<p><strong>2. Product endpoints.</strong> GET /api/products — list all (paginated if the PA requires). GET /api/products/{id}. POST /api/products. PUT /api/products/{id}. DELETE /api/products/{id}. ProductController, similar shape.</p>
<p><strong>3. Cart endpoints.</strong> GET /api/customers/{id}/cart — get the current cart. POST /api/customers/{id}/cart/items — add item. DELETE /api/customers/{id}/cart/items/{itemId} — remove. Cart is owned by Customer; URLs reflect that ownership.</p>
<p><strong>4. Checkout endpoint.</strong> POST /api/customers/{id}/checkout (or similar). Body may be empty (uses the customer's current cart) or include checkout-specific data. Triggers CheckoutService. Returns the created Order with status 201.</p>
<p><strong>5. Order endpoints.</strong> GET /api/customers/{id}/orders — list customer's orders. GET /api/orders/{id} — fetch one order. Customer-scoped vs absolute URLs depending on PA spec.</p>
<p><strong>6. Path parameter naming.</strong> /customers/{id} — id is the customer's ID. Be consistent: don't use {customerId} in some places and {id} in others. The Angular front end expects exact paths; even a one-character mismatch breaks integration.</p>
<p><strong>7. Testing endpoints with Postman.</strong> Send each endpoint a test request via Postman before connecting Angular. Verify status codes, response bodies, and error cases match the spec. Faster than debugging through the Angular UI.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Mismatched URL paths between back-end implementation and Angular expectations — UI calls return 404.</li>
 <li>Returning the wrong status code (200 instead of 201 on POST) — passes naive tests, fails rubric.</li>
 <li>Skipping error cases — the rubric tests 'what happens when customer doesn't exist' and similar.</li>
 <li>Returning lazy-loaded entities directly without considering Jackson serialization — circular reference errors at runtime.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Implement the exact endpoints the PA specifies — paths, methods, request/response shapes, status codes. Standard REST resource shape per entity (list, get, create, update, delete). Cart and order endpoints often customer-scoped. Test each with Postman before connecting Angular. Match the spec down to the path parameter name; small mismatches break integration.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">REST endpoint</span></dt>
 <dd>Specific URL + HTTP method combination handled by a controller method.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Resource URL</span></dt>
 <dd>URL identifying a resource (e.g., /customers/42).</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Path parameter</span></dt>
 <dd>URL segment captured by @PathVariable (e.g., {id} in /customers/{id}).</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Postman</span></dt>
 <dd>Tool for sending HTTP requests to test API endpoints without a UI.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Customer-scoped endpoint</span></dt>
 <dd>URL nested under a parent resource (e.g., /customers/{id}/orders).</dd>
</dl><i data-wgub2_D288_ch5_s4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — springdoc-openapi in a Boot project</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk468a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">OpenAPI</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the specification (formerly Swagger)</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">springdoc-openapi</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the library that generates it for Spring Boot</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">dependency</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">generated from</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">your controllers and annotations</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">/v3/api-docs</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the machine-readable JSON spec</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">/swagger-ui.html</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the interactive documentation page</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Document a Spring Boot API with <strong>OpenAPI/Swagger via springdoc-openapi</strong>: add the <code>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</code> dependency and the docs are generated from your existing controller annotations. Visiting <code>/swagger-ui.html</code> gives a page where each endpoint can be tried interactively — useful evidence for a performance assessment.</div>"},"D288/ch6/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">MySQL Setup &amp; Workbench</text><rect x="282" y="18" width="76" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">BIGINT</text><rect x="484" y="64" width="87" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">VARCHAR(n)</text><rect x="470" y="156" width="115" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">AUTO_INCREMENT</text><rect x="234" y="202" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">FOREIGN KEY constraint</text><rect x="26" y="156" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">MySQL Community Server</text><rect x="37" y="64" width="151" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Creating a database</text></svg></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>MySQL is the database for the D288 PA. Setting it up locally — installing the server, configuring users, connecting from MySQL Workbench and Spring Boot — is the first practical step. Setup confusion is one of the most common reasons students lose hours before any code is written.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. MySQL Community Server.</strong> Free, open-source database. Download from mysql.com or via package manager (Homebrew, apt). The PA assumes a recent version (8.0+). Default port: 3306. Default install creates a root user.</p>
<p><strong>2. MySQL Workbench — the GUI client.</strong> Separate download from MySQL itself. Lets you connect, browse schemas, run queries, edit tables visually. Use it to verify the database is up, see what JPA created, and inspect data without writing SQL by hand.</p>
<p><strong>3. Creating a database.</strong> CREATE DATABASE appdb; (the typical PA database name). USE appdb; switches to it. The database is the container for the PA's tables. Each Spring Boot app typically uses one database.</p>
<p><strong>4. Creating a user with privileges.</strong> CREATE USER 'appuser'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'StrongP@ssword'; GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON appdb.* TO 'appuser'@'localhost'; FLUSH PRIVILEGES;. Don't use root for application access — create a dedicated user.</p>
<p><strong>5. Connection from MySQL Workbench.</strong> Open Workbench → New Connection → Host: localhost, Port: 3306, Username: appuser → enter password → Test Connection. Once connected, browse the schema and run queries.</p>
<p><strong>6. JDBC connection string.</strong> jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/appdb?useSSL=false&serverTimezone=UTC. The string Spring Boot uses to connect. Parameters control SSL, timezone, character set, etc. The PA's specific URL is in the lab instructions or skeleton's application.properties.</p>
<p><strong>7. Verifying connectivity.</strong> Before running Spring Boot: connect via Workbench AND run a query. If Workbench can't connect, neither will Spring Boot. If Workbench connects but Spring Boot doesn't, the application.properties is misconfigured. Isolate the failure layer.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Using the root user for the application — works but bad practice; rubric may notice.</li>
 <li>Connecting to the wrong port — sometimes 3306 conflicts with another service.</li>
 <li>Wrong character set or timezone in the JDBC URL — silent issues with non-ASCII data or date-time fields.</li>
 <li>Workbench connects from your IDE's machine but Spring Boot runs in a container — different localhost; need explicit hostname.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Install MySQL Community Server. Use MySQL Workbench for GUI access. Create the appdb database and a dedicated user with privileges. Connect from Workbench to verify. Spring Boot connects via JDBC URL with the same credentials. Workbench is your sanity check — connect successfully there before debugging Spring Boot connection issues.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">MySQL</span></dt>
 <dd>Open-source relational database used for the D288 PA.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">MySQL Workbench</span></dt>
 <dd>GUI client for connecting to MySQL, browsing schemas, and running queries.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Database</span></dt>
 <dd>Logical container for tables, indexes, and other database objects.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">JDBC URL</span></dt>
 <dd>Connection string identifying the database server, port, database, and parameters.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">GRANT PRIVILEGES</span></dt>
 <dd>MySQL command authorizing a user to perform operations on a database.</dd>
</dl><p>Schema choices the PA grades: <strong>BIGINT</strong> for large numeric IDs, <strong>VARCHAR(n)</strong> for variable-length text. <strong>AUTO_INCREMENT</strong> lets the database assign the next ID automatically on insert. A <strong>FOREIGN KEY constraint</strong> enforces referential integrity — a child row cannot point at a parent that does not exist.</p>"},"D288/ch6/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Database Schema &amp; DDL</text><rect x="276" y="18" width="87" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">DROP TABLE</text><rect x="470" y="64" width="115" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">TRUNCATE TABLE</text><rect x="490" y="156" width="76" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">DELETE</text><rect x="245" y="202" width="151" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">CREATE TABLE syntax</text><rect x="62" y="156" width="101" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Primary keys</text><rect x="62" y="64" width="101" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Foreign keys</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-ddl"><h3>📖 Worked example — reading the schema Hibernate builds</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — entity to DDL, side by side</div><pre>@Entity                              CREATE TABLE customer (
public class Customer {                id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  @Id @GeneratedValue                  name VARCHAR(255),
  private Long id;                     division_id BIGINT,
  private String name;                 FOREIGN KEY (division_id)
  @ManyToOne                             REFERENCES division(id)
  private Division division;          );
}</pre>With ddl-auto=update, Hibernate emits this DDL for you: field → column, @Id+@GeneratedValue → auto-increment primary key, @ManyToOne → foreign-key column. Verify in MySQL with <code>SHOW TABLES;</code> and <code>DESCRIBE customer;</code> — matching what you expected is the point of the exercise.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ update NEVER DROPS</div>ddl-auto=update adds tables/columns but won&rsquo;t remove or retype old ones — renamed fields leave orphan columns behind. Fresh start in dev: create-drop (and NEVER any auto-DDL against a production database).</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>DDL (Data Definition Language) is how database structure is created and modified. The PA may give you a SQL script to run, or expect you to read existing tables. Either way, knowing CREATE TABLE, primary keys, foreign keys, and constraints is essential for understanding what JPA is mapping to.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. CREATE TABLE syntax.</strong> CREATE TABLE customers (id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL, email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE, created_at DATETIME DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);. Each column has a name, type, and optional constraints. PRIMARY KEY uniquely identifies rows.</p>
<p><strong>2. Primary keys.</strong> Every table needs one. Usually a single auto-incrementing integer column (id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT). Composite keys (multiple columns) for join tables. The primary key is what enables lookups, joins, and JPA's @Id mapping.</p>
<p><strong>3. Foreign keys.</strong> FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(id). Enforces that customer_id values must exist in customers. The database refuses inserts that violate it — no orphaned orders. JPA's @ManyToOne maps to a foreign key column.</p>
<p><strong>4. NOT NULL and UNIQUE.</strong> NOT NULL prevents null values in a column. UNIQUE enforces that no two rows share a value. Email columns typically NOT NULL UNIQUE. Maps to JPA's nullable=false and unique=true.</p>
<p><strong>5. Indexes basics.</strong> INDEX (column) speeds up lookups on that column. Primary keys and unique constraints automatically have indexes. Foreign keys often should be indexed for join performance. Don't over-index — each index slows writes.</p>
<p><strong>6. CHECK constraints.</strong> CHECK (price >= 0) enforces a column-level rule. MySQL 8 honors them; older versions parsed but ignored them. Useful for invariants (positive prices, valid status enums).</p>
<p><strong>7. AUTO_INCREMENT and DEFAULTs.</strong> AUTO_INCREMENT generates sequential IDs on insert. DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP fills in created_at automatically. ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP refreshes a 'last modified' column. Cleaner than handling these in application code.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Forgetting foreign key constraints — orphan records accumulate over time.</li>
 <li>Wrong column types (VARCHAR(50) for emails) — silent truncation.</li>
 <li>Indexes on every column — write performance suffers.</li>
 <li>Manually editing schemas while ddl-auto=update is on — JPA may add/drop based on entity changes.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>DDL creates and modifies database structure. CREATE TABLE with primary keys, columns with types and constraints, foreign keys for relationships, indexes for performance. NOT NULL and UNIQUE enforce invariants. AUTO_INCREMENT and DEFAULTs simplify common patterns. Read the PA's schema (or the one JPA generates) carefully — it's the truth your entities must match.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">DDL (Data Definition Language)</span></dt>
 <dd>SQL subset for creating and modifying database structure (CREATE, ALTER, DROP).</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Primary key</span></dt>
 <dd>Column or columns uniquely identifying each row.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Foreign key</span></dt>
 <dd>Column referencing the primary key of another table; enforces referential integrity.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">NOT NULL constraint</span></dt>
 <dd>Prevents null values in a column.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Index</span></dt>
 <dd>Database structure speeding up lookups on a column at the cost of slower writes.</dd>
</dl><p>Three deletions, three meanings: <strong>DROP TABLE</strong> permanently deletes the table AND all its data — structure gone. <strong>TRUNCATE TABLE</strong> removes all rows fast (faster than DELETE) and typically resets auto-increment, but keeps the structure. <strong>DELETE</strong> removes rows one by one, supports WHERE, and participates in transactions/rollback.</p>"},"D288/ch6/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Connecting Spring Boot to…</text><rect x="262" y="18" width="115" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">serverTimezone</text><rect x="470" y="64" width="115" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Spring profile</text><rect x="477" y="156" width="101" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">@DataJpaTest</text><rect x="252" y="202" width="137" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Hibernate dialect</text><rect x="37" y="156" width="151" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">ddl-auto strategies</text><rect x="51" y="64" width="122" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">spring.jpa.show</text></svg></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Spring Boot to MySQL is the most common configuration pain point. Get the application.properties right and Hibernate creates and queries tables seamlessly. Get any property wrong — URL, driver, dialect, ddl-auto — and confusing errors stack up.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. The four essential datasource properties.</strong> spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/appdb. spring.datasource.username=appuser. spring.datasource.password=StrongP@ssword. spring.datasource.driver-class-name=com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver. (Driver class is auto-detected from the URL in modern Spring Boot but explicit doesn't hurt.)</p>
<p><strong>2. Hibernate dialect.</strong> spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.MySQL8Dialect (or MySQLDialect for older versions). Tells Hibernate which SQL flavor to emit. Wrong dialect → SQL that doesn't run on your version → cryptic failures.</p>
<p><strong>3. ddl-auto strategies.</strong> none — Hibernate doesn't touch schema. validate — confirms entity-table match, fails on mismatch. update — adds missing tables/columns (DOESN'T REMOVE). create — drops and recreates at startup. create-drop — create at start, drop at shutdown. For PA dev: validate or update. NEVER create or update in production.</p>
<p><strong>4. spring.jpa.show-sql for debugging.</strong> spring.jpa.show-sql=true logs every SQL statement Hibernate emits. Combined with spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.format_sql=true, the output is readable. Essential when entities aren't behaving as expected — the SQL tells you why.</p>
<p><strong>5. MySQL connector dependency.</strong> pom.xml needs &lt;dependency&lt;groupIdcom.mysql&lt;/groupId&lt;artifactIdmysql-connector-j&lt;/artifactId&lt;scoperuntime&lt;/scope&lt;/dependency. (Or mysql-connector-java for older versions — but mysql-connector-j is current.)</p>
<p><strong>6. Common connection errors.</strong> 'Communications link failure' — server not running or wrong port. 'Access denied' — wrong username/password. 'Unknown database' — database doesn't exist; CREATE it first. 'Public key retrieval not allowed' — add allowPublicKeyRetrieval=true&useSSL=false to URL.</p>
<p><strong>7. Schema initialization with data.sql.</strong> Place schema.sql or data.sql in src/main/resources. Spring Boot runs them at startup (after Hibernate's schema management if ddl-auto creates tables). Useful for seeding reference data — divisions, countries, sample products. The PA's starter often uses this pattern.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Wrong dialect — emits SQL incompatible with your MySQL version.</li>
 <li>ddl-auto=create on a database with existing data — wipes your work every restart.</li>
 <li>Missing the MySQL connector dependency — startup fails with 'no driver class.'</li>
 <li>URL pointing to localhost while Spring Boot runs in a Docker container — host.docker.internal or container networking needed.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Configure datasource URL, username, password, and driver in application.properties. Set Hibernate dialect to match your MySQL version. ddl-auto=update or validate for the PA. Use spring.jpa.show-sql for debugging. Include the mysql-connector-j dependency. Seed reference data via data.sql when needed. Most connection problems trace to one of these properties; verify them systematically.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">spring.datasource</span></dt>
 <dd>Property prefix for database connection settings.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Hibernate dialect</span></dt>
 <dd>Class telling Hibernate which SQL syntax variant to emit.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">ddl-auto</span></dt>
 <dd>Setting controlling Hibernate's schema management on startup.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">mysql-connector-j</span></dt>
 <dd>MySQL JDBC driver dependency for Spring Boot apps.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">data.sql</span></dt>
 <dd>Resource file Spring Boot runs after schema initialization to seed data.</dd>
</dl><p>Environment plumbing: the JDBC URL’s <strong>serverTimezone</strong> parameter prevents timezone errors with MySQL. Use a different <strong>Spring profile</strong> per environment (dev/test/prod) so config switches without code edits. <strong>@DataJpaTest</strong> tests your repository layer against an embedded database — fast, isolated persistence tests.</p>"},"D288/ch7/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">PA Setup &amp; Lab Environment</text><rect x="241" y="18" width="158" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">spring-boot-devtools</text><rect x="481" y="64" width="94" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">breakpoints</text><rect x="445" y="156" width="165" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">stack traces top-down</text><rect x="282" y="202" width="76" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">console</text><rect x="17" y="156" width="190" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Cloning the GitLab starter</text><rect x="33" y="64" width="158" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Running the back end</text></svg></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>The PA can be done in 's hosted lab or in a local IntelliJ + MySQL setup. Each has setup quirks. Knowing the standard pitfalls before starting saves hours and prevents 'the lab seems broken' panic.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. The lab vs local setup.</strong> Lab: pre-configured IntelliJ, MySQL, Postman in a browser-based VM. Pro: no install needed. Con: slower, occasional disconnects. Local: install IntelliJ Community/Ultimate, MySQL, Postman, Node.js (for Angular). Pro: faster, your environment. Con: setup time, version mismatches.</p>
<p><strong>2. Cloning the GitLab starter.</strong> provides a starter repo. git clone the URL into a local folder (or the lab home). Open in IntelliJ. The starter contains: Spring Boot back end (likely partial), Angular front end, README, schema or data SQL. Read the README first.</p>
<p><strong>3. Importing as a Maven project.</strong> IntelliJ recognizes pom.xml — open the folder, IntelliJ imports as a Maven project, downloads dependencies. First-time import takes minutes; subsequent opens are fast. If imports fail, check Maven settings and JDK version.</p>
<p><strong>4. Setting up the local database.</strong> If PA expects MySQL, follow ch6/s1: install, create database, create user. Apply provided SQL scripts (typically in src/main/resources). Verify in Workbench before running Spring Boot.</p>
<p><strong>5. Running the back end.</strong> Right-click the @SpringBootApplication class → Run. Watch logs for 'Started ApplicationName in X seconds.' If it fails, the log shows why — read the stack trace.</p>
<p><strong>6. Running the Angular front end.</strong> Open the angular folder in a terminal. npm install (downloads node_modules). npm start (runs ng serve, typically on port 4200). Browser opens to http://localhost:4200. The Angular app calls the Spring Boot back end on port 8080.</p>
<p><strong>7. Common setup pitfalls.</strong> Wrong Java version (PA expects 17 or 21; check). Wrong Node.js version. Port 8080 already in use. Maven proxy or repository misconfiguration. CORS errors when Angular calls Spring Boot — fixable with @CrossOrigin or a global CORS config.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Skipping the README and starting to code — you miss critical setup steps.</li>
 <li>Wrong Java version — Spring Boot 3 requires 17+; older versions cause compile errors.</li>
 <li>CORS errors when Angular calls Spring Boot — needs explicit CORS configuration.</li>
 <li>Running the back end without the front end (or vice versa) when testing flows — half the system isn't there.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>PA can be done in 's lab or locally. Clone the starter from GitLab. Import as Maven in IntelliJ. Set up MySQL with the provided scripts. Run the Spring Boot back end and Angular front end in parallel. Watch for Java version, Node version, port conflicts, and CORS errors — these are the recurring setup pitfalls. Read the README first.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term"> lab</span></dt>
 <dd>'s hosted, browser-based development environment with pre-installed tools.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Starter project</span></dt>
 <dd>Skeleton code provided by your school as the PA's starting point.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Maven import</span></dt>
 <dd>IntelliJ's process of recognizing pom.xml and downloading dependencies.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">ng serve</span></dt>
 <dd>Angular CLI command running the front end's development server on port 4200.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">CORS</span></dt>
 <dd>Cross-Origin Resource Sharing; controls which origins can call your API.</dd>
</dl><p><strong>spring-boot-devtools</strong> auto-restarts the application when classpath files change — save and refresh. Debug by setting <strong>breakpoints</strong> and stepping through request handling rather than sprinkling prints. Read <strong>stack traces top-down</strong>: the first frames name the failure, the “Caused by” at the bottom names the root. By default the application logs to the <strong>console</strong>, where startup errors (port in use, missing bean) announce themselves.</p>"},"D288/ch7/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the key terms of this section</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="33" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="528" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="320" y2="217" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="171" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><line x1="320" y1="125" x2="112" y2="79" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-opacity="0.7"/><rect x="245" y="102" width="150" height="46" rx="10" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.14" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="320" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">Testing with Postman &amp;…</text><rect x="282" y="18" width="76" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="37" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">201</text><rect x="490" y="64" width="76" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">400</text><rect x="490" y="156" width="76" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="528" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Content</text><rect x="245" y="202" width="151" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="320" y="221" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">params are appended</text><rect x="26" y="156" width="172" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="175" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">assertions with Gradle</text><rect x="33" y="64" width="158" height="30" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="112" y="83" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Postman fundamentals</text></svg></div></div><div class="vl" data-deepdive="d288-we-testing"><h3>📖 Worked example — testing the same endpoint two ways</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — Postman first, then JUnit</div><b>Postman (manual):</b> POST http://localhost:8080/api/customers with body {"name":"Amara","email":"a@x.com"} → expect <b>201</b> and the JSON echo with an id. Then GET /api/customers/1 → 200. Then POST {"name":""} → expect <b>400</b> with the validation message. Three requests = happy path, read-back, and rejection.<br><br><b>JUnit (automated, lives forever):</b><br><pre>@Test
void rejectsBlankName() {
    assertThrows(ConstraintViolationException.class,
                 () -&gt; service.save(new Customer("")));
}
@Test
void savesAndFinds() {
    Customer c = service.save(new Customer("Amara"));
    assertTrue(repo.findById(c.getId()).isPresent());
}</pre>Postman proves it works NOW; JUnit proves it still works after every future change. The PA wants evidence of both.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ TEST THE SAD PATH</div>One passing happy-path test is half a test suite. Graders (and bugs) live in the 400s: blank fields, missing ids, negative quantities — write the assertion that expects the failure.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Testing happens at two levels: API testing (Postman) for end-to-end behavior and unit testing (JUnit) for service logic. Both serve the rubric — and your sanity. Tests caught early are cheap; bugs found in the rubric review are expensive.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Postman fundamentals.</strong> Postman is a desktop app for sending HTTP requests. Create a request: method (GET, POST, etc.), URL (http://localhost:8080/api/customers), headers, body (JSON for POST/PUT). Send. Inspect response: status, body, time.</p>
<p><strong>2. Building a Postman collection.</strong> Group related requests into a collection: Customers, Products, Cart, Checkout, Orders. Each request named clearly. Saves environment variables (base URL). Re-runnable across sessions. Excellent way to verify the PA's required endpoints work.</p>
<p><strong>3. Testing each PA endpoint.</strong> For each endpoint: send a happy-path request, verify status and body. Send an error case (invalid input, nonexistent ID), verify the right status and error message. The PA rubric explicitly checks both happy and error cases for many endpoints.</p>
<p><strong>4. JUnit 5 basics.</strong> @Test annotates a test method. assertEquals(expected, actual) checks equality. assertNotNull, assertTrue, assertThrows — the standard assertions. JUnit runs each test method in isolation. Test classes typically named XxxTest matching the class under test.</p>
<p><strong>5. @SpringBootTest for integration tests.</strong> @SpringBootTest spins up the full Spring application context. Slow but realistic — tests interact with real beans, real database (often H2 in-memory for tests), real services. Use for end-to-end behavior tests.</p>
<p><strong>6. @DataJpaTest for repository tests.</strong> @DataJpaTest configures only the JPA layer with an in-memory test database. Faster than @SpringBootTest. TestEntityManager helps set up test data. Use for testing custom repository queries and JPA mappings.</p>
<p><strong>7. Mocking with Mockito.</strong> @MockBean creates a mock bean Spring injects in place of the real one. when(mock.method(any())).thenReturn(value) defines mock behavior. Useful for testing services in isolation — mock the repository, exercise the service, verify behavior.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Testing only happy paths — the rubric checks error cases too.</li>
 <li>Using @SpringBootTest for everything — slow test runs discourage running tests at all.</li>
 <li>Tests that depend on test order — flaky failures, hard to debug.</li>
 <li>Hardcoded test data that breaks when reference data changes — use test fixtures or @BeforeEach setup.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Postman tests the API end-to-end. Build a collection of all PA endpoints, with happy and error cases. JUnit tests Java code at the unit and integration levels. @SpringBootTest for full-context tests; @DataJpaTest for repositories; Mockito for mocking dependencies in service tests. Cover each rubric requirement with at least one test. Tests catch bugs before the rubric reviewer does.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Postman</span></dt>
 <dd>Desktop app for testing HTTP APIs by sending crafted requests.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">JUnit 5</span></dt>
 <dd>Java testing framework; @Test annotation marks test methods.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@SpringBootTest</span></dt>
 <dd>Test annotation booting the full Spring application context.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@DataJpaTest</span></dt>
 <dd>Test annotation configuring only the JPA layer for fast repository tests.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Mockito</span></dt>
 <dd>Mocking library used to replace real beans with mocks in tests.</dd>
</dl><p>API testing details: send the <strong>Content-Type: application/json</strong> header with JSON bodies. Query <strong>params are appended</strong> to the URL as ?key=value&amp;key2=value2. Run automated <strong>assertions with Gradle</strong> tests so the endpoints stay verified on every build.</p>"},"D288/ch7/s3":{"body":"
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Submission is the last step and the most preventable place to lose points. The rubric is detailed; the checklist is exhaustive. A first-pass submission needs you to verify every requirement is met BEFORE clicking submit, not after the reviewer flags a missing piece.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Re-read the rubric.</strong> Re-read the rubric one more time before submitting. Every row is a check. 'Implemented endpoint X' — yes, you implemented it; tested it; verified it returns the right shape. Many students lose points on items they technically did but didn't double-check at the end.</p>
<p><strong>2. Run end-to-end through Angular.</strong> Don't just hit Postman. Run the Angular front end, click through the full user journey: create customer, browse products, add to cart, checkout, view orders. Each click that returns an error is a defect to fix.</p>
<p><strong>3. Verify each PA task.</strong> PA tasks are typically labeled A through F. Each has acceptance criteria. Walk through each task, verify the criteria, check off in your notes. 'Task A: customer CRUD endpoints — verified GET, POST, PUT, DELETE all return correct status.' Concrete checks, not vague feelings.</p>
<p><strong>4. Take required screenshots.</strong> PA usually requires screenshots: API responses in Postman, the running Angular app, MySQL Workbench showing the data. Take them deliberately, with clear filenames. Cropped, readable, showing what the rubric asks for.</p>
<p><strong>5. Clean up the code.</strong> Run the formatter (IntelliJ has one built-in). Remove System.out.println debug statements. Remove commented-out code. Remove unused imports. Reviewers notice — clean code is faster to review and signals professionalism.</p>
<p><strong>6. Verify the GitLab push.</strong> Push the final code to the GitLab. Check that the master/main branch has all your changes. Open the GitLab UI in a browser to verify. The PA reviewer often pulls from GitLab — what's there is what's graded.</p>
<p><strong>7. Submission packaging.</strong> Follow the PA's exact submission format. Sometimes it's just a GitLab URL. Sometimes it's a zip of the code plus a PDF write-up plus screenshots. Re-read the submission instructions before zipping. Submitting the wrong format gets returned.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Submitting without one final end-to-end run — late-stage breaks (typo in template, wrong route) are common.</li>
 <li>Forgetting to push the latest code to GitLab — the reviewer pulls an older version.</li>
 <li>Including target/ build folder, IDE files (.idea), or local credentials in the zip.</li>
 <li>Skimming the rubric vs reading it carefully — small misunderstandings cost rubric rows.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Re-read the rubric. Run Angular + Spring Boot end-to-end one more time. Verify every PA task against its criteria. Take required screenshots. Clean up the code (formatter, no debug prints, no dead code, no commented blocks). Push to GitLab. Submit in the exact format the PA requires. The submission step is preventable point loss.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">PA rubric</span></dt>
 <dd>Detailed grading checklist for the Performance Assessment.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Acceptance criteria</span></dt>
 <dd>Specific verifiable conditions for completing a PA task.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">GitLab push</span></dt>
 <dd>Sending committed code to the remote the school's repository.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">End-to-end test</span></dt>
 <dd>Test exercising the full system from user input through to data storage.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Submission packaging</span></dt>
 <dd>Format and contents required when submitting the PA (GitLab URL, zip, screenshots).</dd>
</dl><p>PA submission craft: record your walkthrough with a <strong>screen recorder (QuickTime on macOS)</strong>, narrate the <strong>project structure</strong> — controllers, services, repositories, entities — then demo each requirement working. Cite any <strong>sources and tutorials</strong> you leaned on; attribution is part of academic integrity.</p><i data-wgub2_D288_ch7_s3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what has to be disclosed</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 214" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk469a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="14" y="22" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="39" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">tutorial code</text><text x="280" y="39" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">cite it, even a few adapted lines</text><rect x="14" y="52" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">documentation examples</text><text x="280" y="69" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">cite it</text><rect x="14" y="82" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="99" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Stack Overflow answers</text><text x="280" y="99" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">cite it</text><rect x="14" y="112" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="129" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">AI-generated code</text><text x="280" y="129" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">disclose it — WGU permits use with disclosure</text><rect x="14" y="142" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">the standing rule</text><text x="280" y="159" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">you must be able to explain every line you submit</text><rect x="14" y="172" width="250" height="24" rx="5" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="24" y="189" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">not citing</text><text x="280" y="189" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">is an academic integrity violation, not a style slip</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Cite <strong>any code or ideas taken from tutorials, documentation or AI tools</strong> in your references. WGU allows AI as a tool but requires disclosure — and, separately, requires that you understand what you submitted. Code you cannot explain is a problem at evaluation regardless of where it came from.</div>"},"D288/pa_sim/sim":{"body":"<h2>Practice Assessment — 50-Question Simulation</h2><p>Fifty questions curated from every unit of this course for full-breadth coverage. Take it timed (75 minutes), closed-book, on your scratch space. Review every miss — each explanation names the exact rule tested.</p><i data-simfresh="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">✓ FRESH QUESTIONS</div>Every one of these 50 questions is one you have <b>not answered before</b>, so the score is a real read on where you stand.</div>"},"D288/oa_sim/sim":{"body":"<h2>Objective Assessment — 50-Question Simulation</h2><p>Led by this course’s exam-calibrated final bank, filled to 50 with zero overlap against your PA. Timed, closed-book. Score 85%+ here and you are walking into the real OA over-prepared.</p><i data-simfresh="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">✓ FRESH QUESTIONS</div>Every one of these 66 questions is one you have <b>not answered before</b>, so the score is a real read on where you stand.</div>"},"D288/weak_drill/sim":{"body":"<h2>🎯 Weak Spots</h2><p>A practice set drawn from the <strong>topics you score lowest on</strong> — computed live from your quiz history (any topic under 80% accuracy, weakest first). The more you practice, the more precisely this targets your gaps. No history in this course yet? It starts you on the hardest questions until it learns where you struggle.</p>"},"D288/review_missed/sim":{"body":"<h2>🔁 Review Missed</h2><p>Every question you’ve <strong>answered incorrectly</strong> in this course, gathered in one place so you can retry them (most-recent misses first, up to 30). Get one right here and you’ll see the explanation reinforce the exact rule tested. Nothing missed yet? It falls back to this course’s hardest questions.</p>"},"D288/gapfill/g1":{"body":"<h3>DTO mapping and response shaping</h3>
<div class="vl" data-figure="auto" style="margin:18px 0;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the steps, in order</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 94" style="width:100%; max-width:640px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fa" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text-3)"/></marker></defs><rect x="12" y="10" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.12" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="20" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">1</text><text x="71" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">DTOs are faster</text><text x="71" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">to persist</text><line x1="130" y1="38" x2="164" y2="38" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="166" y="10" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="174" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">2</text><text x="225" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">To expose only</text><text x="225" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">intended fields…</text><line x1="284" y1="38" x2="318" y2="38" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="320" y="10" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="328" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">3</text><text x="379" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Entities can't be</text><text x="379" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">serialized</text><line x1="438" y1="38" x2="472" y2="38" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#fa)"/><rect x="474" y="10" width="118" height="56" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="482" y="24" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700">4</text><text x="533" y="40" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">DTOs replace the</text><text x="533" y="54" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">database</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout callout-analogy"><div class="callout-label">💡 IN PLAIN ENGLISH</div><p>A <strong>DTO</strong> (Data Transfer Object) is a plain object that carries exactly the data your API should send or receive — no more. It sits between your JPA <strong>entities</strong> (tied to the database) and the outside world, so you never leak internal fields or trip over lazy-loaded relationships.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-recall"><div class="callout-label">⚙️ WHAT'S REALLY HAPPENING</div><p><strong>Why not just return the entity?</strong> Entities carry everything — password hashes, internal IDs, bidirectional relationships that cause infinite JSON loops or <code>LazyInitializationException</code>. A DTO exposes only the fields the client needs, in the shape the client wants.</p><p><strong>Mapping</strong> is converting entity ↔ DTO. You can do it <em>manually</em> (a constructor or a <code>toDto()</code> method copying fields) or with a <em>library</em> like <strong>MapStruct</strong> (compile-time generated mappers, fast, type-safe) or <strong>ModelMapper</strong> (runtime reflection, less boilerplate).</p></div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — entity vs DTO</div><table><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Entity (Customer)</th><th>CustomerDTO</th></tr><tr><td>Fields</td><td>id, name, email, passwordHash, orders, address, createdAt</td><td>id, name, email</td></tr><tr><td>Used for</td><td>persistence (JPA/DB)</td><td>API request/response</td></tr><tr><td>Risk if exposed directly</td><td>leaks passwordHash, lazy-loading & JSON cycles</td><td>none — only safe fields</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="callout callout-tip"><div class="callout-label">📝 EXAM WORDING</div><p><span class="key-term">DTO</span> = shapes data for transfer, decoupling the API from the persistence model. It prevents <span class="key-term">over-exposure</span> of sensitive fields and avoids lazy-loading/JSON-cycle problems. <span class="key-term">MapStruct</span> = compile-time mapper; <span class="key-term">ModelMapper</span> = runtime mapper; or map by hand in the service layer.</p></div>
<div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 OA-STYLE CHECK</div>Why return a DTO instead of the JPA entity from a REST endpoint?<ol type="A"><li>DTOs are faster to persist</li><li>To expose only intended fields and avoid leaking internal/lazy data</li><li>Entities can't be serialized</li><li>DTOs replace the database</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>B.</b> A DTO exposes only the fields the client needs, decoupling the API from the entity and avoiding leaked fields, lazy-loading errors, and JSON cycles.</div></details></div>"},"D288/pa_fresh/sim":{"body":"<h2>🆕 Fresh Questions — Brand-New Practice</h2><p>40 brand-new questions written for this course and mapped across its competencies — not repeated anywhere else in the app. Take it timed and closed-book, then review every miss.</p><i data-simfresh="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">✓ FRESH QUESTIONS</div>Every one of these 40 questions is one you have <b>not answered before</b>, so the score is a real read on where you stand.</div>"},"D288/aistote/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-visual="aistote-method">
<h3>🧠 The Aistote Method (J-Method)</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE IDEA</div>The Aistote app is built on two of the most evidence-backed study techniques — <b>active recall</b> and <b>spaced repetition</b> — run in short, intense <b>25-minute</b> blocks (its "J-Method"). Instead of slowly re-reading, you spend the time <b>testing yourself</b> and re-testing what you miss. This app already has every tool the method needs.</div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE 3-STEP LOOP</div>
<table><tr><th>Step</th><th>What you do</th><th>Where in this app</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>1. Convert</b></td><td>Turn the material into questions instead of re-reading it.</td><td>Open a section, skim the lesson once, then jump to its <b>Quiz</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>2. Recall</b></td><td>Answer from memory first — no peeking — then check.</td><td>Take the section Quiz, or a <b>PA / OA simulation</b>; rate your confidence.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>3. Space it</b></td><td>Re-test only what you missed, on a growing schedule.</td><td>Every miss becomes a <b>Flashcard</b>; review them on the app's spaced-repetition schedule.</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">A 25-MINUTE J-METHOD SESSION</div>
<table><tr><th>Time</th><th>Do this</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">0–3 min</td><td>Skim the lesson's key terms and the big idea — don't memorize yet, just get the lay of the land.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3–18 min</td><td>Take the section quiz (or a 15-question drill). Try to <b>recall the answer first</b>, reveal it after.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">18–23 min</td><td>Redo <b>only</b> the questions you got wrong.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">23–25 min</td><td>Send the stubborn ones to <b>Flashcards</b> and note tomorrow's quick review.</td></tr></table>
Start the block with the <b>⚡ Focus</b> timer (25 min) on any lesson — that is your J-Method session.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE ONE RULE</div>Always try to answer <b>from memory before you look</b>. The effort of retrieving is what actually builds the memory — re-reading feels productive but barely moves it.</div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHY IT WORKS</div><b>Active recall</b> (testing yourself) forces your brain to reconstruct the answer, which strengthens it far more than recognition. <b>Spaced repetition</b> reviews each fact just as you're about to forget it, moving it into long-term memory with the fewest reps. The 25-minute block simply makes you actually do both, every day.</div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">DAILY RHYTHM</div>Two or three 25-minute J-Method blocks a day (new material in one, missed-question review in the next) beats a single long cram session — and it's how the Aistote method delivers "learn it in 25 minutes" instead of hours.</div>
</div>"},"D288/userdocs/add":{"body":"<h3 style="margin-top:0;">➕ Add a document to this class</h3><p style="font-size:13.5px; color:var(--text-3); line-height:1.6;">Paste your document below (or choose a .txt file). It will be kept <b>word-for-word, exactly as you add it</b>. Word/PDF files: open them, select all, copy, and paste here — that preserves your text precisely.</p><div data-docform="D288" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px; max-width:720px;"><input data-doc-title type="text" placeholder="Title (e.g., Unit 3 Sample Test)" style="padding:11px 13px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid var(--border); background:var(--surface-2); color:var(--text); font-size:14px;"><div style="display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; font-size:13.5px;"><label style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; cursor:pointer;"><input data-doc-type type="radio" name="doctype-D288" value="notes" checked> 📒 Notes (shown as-is)</label><label style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; cursor:pointer;"><input data-doc-type type="radio" name="doctype-D288" value="test"> 📝 Practice test (answers held until the end)</label></div><textarea data-doc-content rows="12" placeholder="Paste the document here, exactly as you want it shown…" style="padding:12px 13px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid var(--border); background:var(--surface-2); color:var(--text); font-size:14px; line-height:1.6; resize:vertical;"></textarea><textarea data-doc-key rows="4" placeholder="(Practice tests only, optional) Paste the ANSWER KEY here — it will be hidden until you open it at the end. You can skip this if your document already has a line saying “Answer Key” before the answers." style="padding:12px 13px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid var(--border); background:var(--surface-2); color:var(--text); font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.6; resize:vertical;"></textarea><div style="display:flex; gap:10px; align-items:center; flex-wrap:wrap;"><label style="font-size:13px; color:var(--text-3); cursor:pointer; padding:9px 13px; border:1px dashed var(--border); border-radius:10px;">📎 Choose .txt file<input data-doc-file type="file" accept=".txt,.md,.csv,text/plain" style="display:none;"></label><button data-doc-save="D288" style="border:none; background:var(--accent, #6366f1); color:#fff; font-weight:700; font-size:14px; padding:11px 20px; border-radius:10px; cursor:pointer;">Save document</button><span data-doc-msg style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-4);"></span></div></div>"},"D288/cheat/c1":{"body":"<h2>📋 Java OOP Foundations</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Java Classes, Objects, Encapsulation</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ WHY IT MATTERS IN A BACK END Entities and DTOs cross layers constantly; if fields were public, any layer could corrupt them. Encapsulation is what makes the service layer the ONLY place business rules live.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Inheritance, Polymorphism, Interfaces</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ PROGRAM TO THE INTERFACE Declaring PriceCalculator calc (not HolidayPricing) is the graded habit: it enables swapping, mocking in tests, and DI. Program to an interface, not an implementation.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Collections, Generics, and Lambdas</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>The collections framework</strong> — Java's java.util package gives you a unified hierarchy of containers:</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>ArrayList — the workhorse</strong> — List&lt;String> names = new ArrayList&lt;>(); names.add("Alice"); names.add("Bob"); names.add("Charlie"); names.size(); // 3 names.get(0); // "Alice" names.contains("Bob"); //…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>HashMap — keyed lookup</strong> — Map&lt;String, Integer> ages = new HashMap&lt;>(); ages.put("Alice", 30); ages.put("Bob", 25); ages.get("Alice"); // 30 ages.get("Carol"); // null (key not present)…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>HashSet — uniqueness</strong> — Set&lt;String> seen = new HashSet&lt;>(); seen.add("Alice"); seen.add("Alice"); // ignored — already present seen.size(); // 1 seen.contains("Alice"); // true HashSet uses…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Generics — type-parameterized containers</strong> — Before generics, collections were untyped (held Object ):</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Defining generic classes and methods</strong> — public class Box&lt;T> { private T value; public void set(T value) { this.value = value; } public T get() { return value; } } Box&lt;String> sb = new Box&lt;>(); sb.set("hello");…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Wildcards</strong> — For method parameters where the exact generic type doesn't matter: use ? extends T to read (producer) and ? super T to write (consumer) — PECS. A plain List&lt;?> works when you only need size and iteration.</p></div>"},"D288/cheat/c2":{"body":"<h2>📋 Spring Boot Fundamentals</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Spring Boot Overview &amp; Project Setup</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Core concepts</strong> — 1. Spring Boot vs Spring Framework. Spring Framework is the core IoC container, plus integrations for web, data, and security. Spring Boot wraps Spring Framework with…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Quick recap</strong> — Spring Boot is Spring Framework + auto-configuration + embedded server + starter dependencies. Generate projects with Spring Initializr. pom.xml lists dependencies;…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Glossary</strong></p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Beans, IoC, Dependency Injection</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>What is "Inversion of Control"?</strong> — Normally, your code creates its dependencies:</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>The Spring IoC container</strong> — Spring's IoC container is the object that holds, configures, and assembles all your beans. You tell it: "Here are my classes. Here's how they fit together. Build me an…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>What is a bean?</strong> — A bean is any object managed by the Spring container. The container handles:</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Stereotype annotations — marking classes as beans</strong> — All of these are FUNCTIONALLY EQUIVALENT (they all just mark the class as a bean) but communicate INTENT. Pick the one that matches the class's role.</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Component scanning</strong> — Spring Boot's @SpringBootApplication includes @ComponentScan , which scans the package containing the main class (and all sub-packages) for stereotype-annotated classes.…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Constructor injection — the preferred form</strong> — @Service public class UserService { private final UserRepository repo; public UserService(UserRepository repo) { // Spring injects here this.repo = repo; } } Why…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Field injection — discouraged</strong> — @Service public class UserService { @Autowired private UserRepository repo; // works but discouraged } Field injection is concise but bad: dependencies aren't final,…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Application Properties &amp; Configuration</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ PORTS: 8080 vs 3306 server.port is YOUR app s HTTP port; 3306 inside the JDBC URL is the DATABASE s port. Mixing them up is the classic can t-connect misdiagnosis.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Spring Boot Project Structure</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ LAYERS ARE A CONVENTION WITH TEETH controller → service → repository, each calling only the next: graders and interviewers both look for it. A controller talking straight to the repository skips validation and business rules, and turns every schema change into a controller rewrite — keep each layer talking only to the layer directly below it.</p></div>"},"D288/cheat/c3":{"body":"<h2>📋 JPA, Hibernate &amp; Entities</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Entity Classes &amp; JPA Annotations</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Core concepts</strong> — 1. @Entity marks a persistent class. @Entity on a class tells JPA this represents a database table. By default, the table name matches the class name (Customer →…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Quick recap</strong> — @Entity marks a class as persistent. @Id picks the primary key. @GeneratedValue auto-generates it (IDENTITY for MySQL). @Column tunes column-level details. Java types…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Glossary</strong></p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Entity Relationships</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ mappedBy NAMES A FIELD, NOT A TABLE mappedBy = "country" refers to the Java FIELD in Division. Typo it and you get a phantom join table and duplicate data. When a relationship doubles, check mappedBy first.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Spring Data JPA Repositories</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ findById RETURNS OPTIONAL Handle absence explicitly: repo.findById(id).orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException(...)) . Calling .get() blind is the NoSuchElementException everyone hits once.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">PA Entity Modeling: E-Commerce ERD</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Core concepts</strong> — 1. Read the ERD before writing entities. The ERD shows tables, columns, primary keys, foreign keys, relationships. Each table maps to one entity class. Each column maps…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Quick recap</strong> — The PA gives you an ERD; map it carefully to entities. Customer at the center. Division/Country supports geography. Product, Cart, CartItem, Order, OrderItem form the…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Glossary</strong> — Money is BigDecimal , never double — binary floating point cannot represent 0.10 exactly and pennies drift. Order totals: either store the total (denormalized — fast to read, must be kept in sync) or compute it from the items on demand (normalized — always consistent, a little slower). Know the trade-off; either is fine if applied consistently.</p></div>"},"D288/cheat/c4":{"body":"<h2>📋 Service Layer &amp; Business Logic</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Service Layer Pattern</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ CONTROLLERS STAY THIN If a controller method contains an if about stock or prices, the design aspect fails. Controller = translate HTTP; service = decide; repository = store.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Validation with Bean Validation</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ @NotBlank vs @NotNull vs @NotEmpty @NotNull allows "" ; @NotEmpty allows " " ; @NotBlank requires actual visible content — for names and required text, NotBlank is almost always the right pick.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">PA Checkout Service Implementation</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Core concepts</strong> — 1. The checkout flow. Receive request: cart ID or cart contents + customer. Validate: cart not empty, products in stock. Calculate: subtotal, tax, total. Create Order…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Quick recap</strong> — Checkout: receive cart, validate, calculate totals (subtotal + tax based on customer's division), create Order and OrderItems, persist atomically (@Transactional),…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Glossary</strong> — Cascading saves: with cascade = ALL on Order’s items relationship, saving the Order persists its items in one operation — the parent owns the children’s lifecycle. Add orphanRemoval = true and an item removed from the collection is deleted too — without it, dropped children linger in the database.</p></div>"},"D288/cheat/c5":{"body":"<h2>📋 REST APIs &amp; Controllers</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">REST Architecture &amp; HTTP Methods</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ STATUS CODES ARE PART OF THE CONTRACT 200 OK, 201 Created (with the new resource), 400 bad input, 404 missing resource, 500 server bug. Returning 200 for a failed lookup is a graded mistake — the code IS the message.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">@RestController &amp; Endpoint Mapping</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ @Controller vs @RestController @Controller returns VIEW NAMES (Thymeleaf pages); @RestController returns DATA. Use the wrong one and you get a 404-ish template error instead of JSON — a favorite what-went-wrong…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Response Handling &amp; Error Responses</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Core concepts</strong> — 1. ResponseEntity for explicit control. ResponseEntity&lt;Customer wraps the response with body, status code, and headers. ResponseEntity.ok(customer) — 200 with body.…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Quick recap</strong> — Use ResponseEntity for explicit control over status and headers; return entity directly for default 200. @ResponseStatus on exception classes maps them to status codes.…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Glossary</strong></p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">PA REST Endpoints Implementation</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ PROVE IT, DON T ASSUME IT The requirement isn t done when the code compiles — it s done when the Postman round-trip shows the status code AND the database state change. Screenshot both for the submission.</p></div>"},"D288/cheat/c6":{"body":"<h2>📋 Exam Coverage: DTOs &amp; Response Shaping</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">DTO Mapping and Response Shaping</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>DTO mapping and response shaping</strong> — A DTO (Data Transfer Object) is a plain object that carries exactly the data your API should send or receive — no more. It sits between your JPA entities (tied to the database schema) and the JSON your API exposes — so you can reshape, hide, or version the response without touching the entity, and entity internals like lazy relations never leak into the API.</p></div>"},"D288/cheat/c7":{"body":"<h2>📋 MySQL &amp; Database Integration</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">MySQL Setup &amp; Workbench</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Core concepts</strong> — 1. MySQL Community Server. Free, open-source database. Download from mysql.com or via package manager (Homebrew, apt). The PA assumes a recent version (8.0+). Default…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Quick recap</strong> — Install MySQL Community Server. Use MySQL Workbench for GUI access. Create the appdb database and a dedicated user with privileges. Connect from Workbench to verify.…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Glossary</strong> — Schema choices the PA grades: BIGINT for large numeric IDs, VARCHAR(n) for variable-length text. AUTO_INCREMENT lets the database assign the next ID automatically on…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Database Schema &amp; DDL</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ update NEVER DROPS ddl-auto=update adds tables/columns but won t remove or retype old ones — renamed fields leave orphan columns behind. Fresh start in dev: create-drop (and NEVER any auto-DDL against a production…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Connecting Spring Boot to MySQL</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Core concepts</strong> — 1. The four essential datasource properties. spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/appdb. spring.datasource.username=appuser.…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Quick recap</strong> — Configure datasource URL, username, password, and driver in application.properties. Set Hibernate dialect to match your MySQL version. ddl-auto=update or validate for…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Glossary</strong> — Environment plumbing: the JDBC URL’s serverTimezone parameter prevents timezone errors with MySQL. Use a different Spring profile per environment (dev/test/prod) so H2 keeps serving tests while MySQL serves production — switch with spring.profiles.active, never by hand-editing the config.</p></div>"},"D288/cheat/c8":{"body":"<h2>📋 PA Project Workflow &amp; Testing</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">PA Setup &amp; Lab Environment</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Core concepts</strong> — 1. The lab vs local setup. Lab: pre-configured IntelliJ, MySQL, Postman in a browser-based VM. Pro: no install needed. Con: slower, occasional disconnects. Local:…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Quick recap</strong> — PA can be done in 's lab or locally. Clone the starter from GitLab. Import as Maven in IntelliJ. Set up MySQL with the provided scripts. Run the Spring Boot back end and…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Glossary</strong> — spring-boot-devtools auto-restarts the application when classpath files change — save and refresh. Debug by setting breakpoints and stepping through request handling…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Testing with Postman &amp; JUnit</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ TEST THE SAD PATH One passing happy-path test is half a test suite. Graders (and bugs) live in the 400s: blank fields, missing ids, negative quantities — write the assertion that expects the failure.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">PA Submission Checklist</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Core concepts</strong> — 1. Re-read the rubric. Re-read the rubric one more time before submitting. Every row is a check. 'Implemented endpoint X' — yes, you implemented it; tested it; verified…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Quick recap</strong> — Re-read the rubric. Run Angular + Spring Boot end-to-end one more time. Verify every PA task against its criteria. Take required screenshots. Clean up the code…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;"><strong>Glossary</strong> — PA submission craft: record your walkthrough with a screen recorder (QuickTime on macOS) , narrate the project structure — controllers, services, repositories, entities — then demo each rubric feature working live. Check your audio, name the file clearly, and show the feature running, not just the code that should run it.</p></div>"}}
